   165	"This is the first day. There are twenty-nine after it. It's stood a spring and six miles of road, and it's no worse than I left it, which is more than you deserved." She sat back. "Stick; binding; no kneeling. No walls. You'll want to carry something tomorrow, when those boxes come off my mule. You're not to."
   166	
   167	"I shall take that under advisement."
   168	
   169	"You'll take it under your arm, with the stick." But she had not said it sharply. She had given him as long as she had given a girl's arm, to the breath, and he had marked it; Cael saw him look after her as she rose, as a man looks who has been handed something across a counter and can find no price on it.
   170	
   171	"You priced him like one of us," said Lira, when the healer came back to the fire. She said it without any edge. She only wished to have it right.
   172	
   173	"A knee's a knee. I told you what I think of that column."
   174	
   175	That left one, and she had kept him for the last.
   176	
   177	She stood before Cael at her stride and a half, and she looked at his hands. They lay on Hesk's book.
   178	
   179	"You, I've not read. I said at my own plank that when I put my hands on that again I'd know what I was reading for, and I'd have slept. I don't know yet, and I've a round in front of me."
   180	
   181	She had begun to push a sleeve up, by old habit. She looked at the hand that was doing it, and drew the sleeve down.
   182	
   183	"That's on me," said Oryn. "It's the one thing I'm walking up that road owing, and I'll pay it."
   184	
   185	"Then it'll keep," said Cael.
   186	
   187	"It'll keep three weeks. I'm not easy about it. I'd sooner you knew that I'm not."
   188	
   189	She took up her bowl from the wall's top, and looked into it, and found it empty.
   190	
   191	"Three weeks, and the mill. And you'll not come to me there with what you think that I ought to hear." Her eyes came up, and went along the six of them, and they were as level as a plank. "Bring me whatever walks out of that archive with you. All of it."
   192	
   193	Cael had a thing in him, since noon, that had walked out of a row of paper and that he had not written down. He heard those three words fall on it; and he said nothing, and was aware of a small grey man at the hearth who said nothing either.
   194	
   195	---
   196	
   197	The boxes came off the mule on the two hundred and eighty-first day, as soon as there was light to rope by, and the frost was white on the fork-stone.
   198	
   199	Brom unroped them himself. He had lashed them on the brown back at a ferry's shed with the beckets outward, so that they might go up any road; he had not said then that the last of that road would be on people's shoulders, and he did not say it now. He took the heaviest on his left side, where the webbing was not.
   200	
   201	Lira had the second up before anybody could offer for it. She got her arms through its beckets, the right and then the left, and stood under it with her chin out.
   202	
   203	"It's a box," she said. "I've two arms. I was told to use them."
   204	
   205	"You were told not to fall on one," said Oryn. She was drawing the mule's girth.
   206	
   207	"Then I'll fall on the box."
   208	
   209	Seln made no move toward the third, and nobody looked for him to. He went thirty yards ahead of any column with his hands empty, since a crew does not load the one person in it whose work is to see what is coming; and he had a case on his chest already that he had never yet set down. The third box stood on the frozen turf.
   210	
   211	A raw hand in linen went out toward it, from the side where a stick was.
   212	
   213	"Twenty-eight," said Oryn, without turning round.
   214	
   215	The hand stopped.
   216	
   217	"I was not about to lift it. I had thought to steady it, while he—"
   218	
   219	"It's steady," said Cael. He got the box up by one becket, and Brom, who had his own on him already, put out his free hand and swung the other home. "It's been steady since Ostrand."
   220	
   221	Vastin put his hand back on the stick. He did not look pleased, and he did not argue; it was a middle state, and Cael thought that the man had not spent much of his life in it.
   222	
   223	There was not a great deal else to be done, since Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them off a landing. The tarred case that held a dead man's cord had come off the mule last of all, and it leaned against the foot of the fork-stone, where Brom had set it. The healer's pannier stayed on the mule, with her linen in it, and a bow hung beside it with its one bolt.
   224	
   225	"She'll look for you at every halt," said Brom. He had the mule's nose in his big palm. "She's had three boxes on her since the snow."
   226	
   227	"She's a mule. She'll look for where the barley came from." Oryn took up the cheek-strap. "I'll tell her that it's gone under a town."
   228	
   229	She did not say goodbye, since she had never said it to anybody, at any gate. She looked at each of them once, in no order, and at a bound knee last. Then she stood a moment before Cael, with her free hand shut, at her own distance.
   230	
   231	"Not the steps," she said. "Not the square."
   232	
   233	She turned the mule's head to the north.
   234	
   235	They watched her go down the rut and past the fold, a square figure in an old coat with a small brown beast beside her. She walked as she walked to any gate, without haste, and she did not look back. Cael had heard why, once, though not from her. A healer who looks back has only doubled what she is carrying.
   236	
   237	"It's six bowls," said Lira.
   238	
   239	"It's seven," said Brom. "I'll lay hers. Somebody can carry it empty for three weeks, and I'd as soon that it were me."
   240	
   241	---
   242	
   243	They took the western ruts. It was Karis who stopped, forty strides up them, so short that Brom all but walked into her; and she looked back at the stone.
   244	
   245	Nobody needed to be told. A tarred case leaned there by itself. She had a flat under one arm and a hide under the other; three backs had a box apiece; and the man who went in front carried nothing, as he had to. On the northern rut the bend of the moor had that moment taken a healer and a mule out of sight.
   246	
   247	Vastin had turned already.
   248	
   249	He went back down to the stone at his own pace. He did not kneel to the thing. He hooked its strap with the head of his stick, and brought it up to his hand, and slung it on the shoulder above his sound leg; and then he came up after them, a step at a time, and stopped where Cael stood.
   250	
   251	"It is a cord, a mallet and one stake," he said. "It weighs less than my pack. I say so, since you will have thought it."
   252	
   253	"She told you that you weren't to carry."
   254	
   255	"She did. I told her that I should take it under advisement, and I have." He did not look away. "I waited until she could not be put to the trouble of forbidding it a third time. I shall not pretend to you that it was better than that. If the knee complains of a strap, I shall set the thing down in the road and say so."
   256	
   257	Cael turned it over. It was not obedience, and it was not quite a cheat. The man had taken the lightest load that the crew owned, on the good side, with nobody asked; and he had then walked up to the one person who kept a book and told him exactly what he had done. Nobody had asked him to carry it. Nobody told him no.
   258	
   259	"She'll have it out of you at the mill," said Cael.
   260	
   261	"I expect that she will."
   262	
   263	The box rode badly on Cael's shoulders for the first furlong, and then he found the way of it, as he had on a stair with something heavier. At a quarter of a mile he turned.
   264	
   265	He had not meant to; but the road had begun to climb, and from where he stood the whole of the fork lay below him in the early light, with the stone at the parting, and the round pen, and a thread of blue going up from its corner where the peats still smouldered. There was nobody on the northern rut for a long way. Then he found them again, very small, beyond the bend of the moor's edge: a dark upright speck and a lower one. They were not hurrying, and neither had turned its head.
   266	
   267	He looked for as long as it took Brom to get twenty strides ahead of him. Then he shifted the box, and went up after the others.
   268	
   269	---
   270	
   271	The rise took them the whole of the forenoon.
   272	
   273	It was a long bare swell of the moor, a mile and more from its foot to its head. The ruts went up it aslant, as ruts do when they have been cut by loaded carts, and the wind came over the top of it into their faces. Nobody spoke, since it was a road, and the hold lay on all of them; and there was a box on three backs, and a man on a stick behind, who would not be waited for and could not be left.
   274	
   275	Cael walked with his eyes on the ruts.
   276	
   277	He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
   278	
   279	And there was a day. He had not written that, and he did not go over it. He only knew where it lay, as a person knows without looking where the stair is in his own house.
   280	
   281	They came to the top a little before noon.
   282	
   283	The ruts went over a lip of bare rock and began to go down; and the whole country opened in front of him, as it had opened once from a shelf, on the second day past the Line, when it was the east that he was looking at.
   284	
   285	This was the west. The moor fell away in long brown folds to a plain, and the plain was green. There were fields on it, with hedges, and he had not seen a hedge for two hundred and eighty days. A river came down out of the north and went across that green in loops, shining, and in the nearest of the loops the land stood up in a bluff.
   286	
   287	There was a town on the bluff.
   288	
   289	It went up in three steps. A wall ran round the foot of the bluff, grey and long, with a great many roofs behind it. Higher, where the ground steepened, there was a second wall, and the roofs inside that were of slate. The third was at the top, close and pale, about the crown of the rock. It was a day's walk off still, and he could have covered the whole of it with his thumb.
   290	
   291	"Three tiers," said Lira, at his shoulder. She had set her box down. "They've built the ladder out of stone. I'd wondered what it would look like from outside."
   292	
   293	Nobody else said anything, and Karis had stopped a yard in front of them all. She stood with a calfskin under her arm and the wind pulling at her coat, and looked at a place whose name she had read once on the spine of a catalogue that she was not let to open. Seln was counting gates, with his lips. Brom lowered his box to the rock and rolled his left shoulder, and studied those three walls as he would have studied any wall.
   294	
   295	There was a light on the crown.
   296	
   297	It stood above the highest roofs, at the very top, where a town of that kind keeps the one building that reads. It was pale, and very small at that distance, and it did not flicker as a fire does. It burned as steadily in the full noon as a lamp burns in a shut room.
   298	
   299	Cael did not look at it.
   300	
   301	He had seen that it was there; and he turned his eyes from it as he would have turned them from a hound at forty yards, without haste, and set them on the lowest wall, and kept them there. It cost him a little; and he found that it was a thing which a person could do, and that it would want doing a good many times.
   302	
   303	A stick came up over the lip behind him, and stopped.
   304	
   305	Vastin stood with both hands on its head, getting his breath, with the bound leg a little out and a tarred case on his shoulder. He looked at the town for some time. He had gone in at those gates, no doubt, on a horse, behind a clerk, with a seal.
   306	
   307	He said nothing, and nobody asked him to.
   308	
   309	Cael turned to Karis. He had not known until he was speaking that he meant to.
   310	
   311	"The oilcloth. Have you got it where you can reach it?"
   312	
   313	She looked at him. Then she shifted the hide, and put her gloved hand into the breast of her coat, and brought out a square of oilcloth no bigger than a man's palm. She opened it on the top of Brom's box, along its own creases, and held down the corner that the wind wanted.
   314	
   315	There was one sheet in it, of the Compact's ordinary desk paper, in a square upright hand. It lay the wrong way up to him. A schedule of housings ran down it, as dull as a tally of sacks; and half way down, between two strokes of a pen, there was a title that nobody had ever met, and the name of the town in front of him.
   316	
   317	He had kept his hand a hand's width from that page for the whole of a lamp, so that he might still say no to it. He had ruled on a slip instead, at a plank, in daylight. He had walked three days up a road behind a job. Somewhere on that road the no had gone, without his having marked the stride; it had gone at a spring, perhaps, between two hands laid flat on a rock.
   318	
   319	He put out his right hand, and laid it flat on the page.
   320	
   321	It was only paper, cold, and a little rough, and the wind moved it under his palm.
   322	
   323	"Did it bite?" said Lira.
   324	
   325	"No."
   326	
   327	"I said that it wouldn't."
   328	
   329	He lifted his hand. Karis folded the cloth over the sheet without a word, and put it back inside her coat, where it had ridden; and her small pale face had nothing in it that could have been written down.
   330	
   331	He got the box onto his shoulders again.
   332	
   333	They went down off the rise in file: a quiet man in front, with his hands empty, and a girl with a box on her back, and a big one with another, and a small one with a hide under her arm. Cael came after them with the third. He had been taken to a great many registries in his life, and sent for by some, and filed in all of them; and he had never before walked toward one because he had chosen to.
   334	
   335	He had gone a dozen strides down the western side before he knew that there was nobody behind him.
   336	
   337	The stick was on his left. It came down on the turf at the rut's edge a little after each of his own strides, and a dead man's cord swung beside it in its tarred case. Nobody had told that man where to walk, on the first day of this road, and he had walked last, and a little too far back. Nobody had told him today. He had only not dropped behind when the file made up; and he was there, a shoulder off, on the ditch side, an old man who had been a marshal and was learning to be nobody.
   338	
   339	Cael did not ask him to. He did not tell him no. He kept his eyes on the lowest wall.

