    47	It stood at the pull-in's western end, where the hedge stopped and the ditch ran on beside the road. He sat down on the bank with his back against the stone and his face to the east. The moor lay along the sky there, a long way off and very dark; they had been on the top of it at noon. The town was behind his shoulder, with whatever lights it had.
    48	
    49	He had found a thing at a brook, and it had seemed to him a large one. But he had found it cold and empty, after a quarter of an hour on his back in a field, and he had learned from Karis what a first count was worth. She never trusted a figure until it had been made a second time, by a person in a different temper. He was in a different temper now. He had a bowl in him, and nine miles, and he was too tired to want anything in particular.
    50	
    51	He put his palm on his breastbone and two fingers on his wrist, and counted.
    52	
    53	It did not take a quarter of an hour, since he knew the road. At ten he had his own gait, slow now, as slow as he had ever felt it; and he went down the hiss of Wind without steering, and let it lean where it leaned. He did not go round a second time. He stopped a long way out, where the leaning began, and stood there, and asked the going-along the one thing that he had come to ask.
