   139	The old man was not looking at him. He was looking at his own right leg, laid out before him like a plank.
   140	
   141	"I did not hope for it. If that plate lit, then I had sat four feet from you for three days, with everything that I have, and had seen nothing of a thing that a sheet of brass could find in eleven seconds. I should have had to ask what my eyes were worth, and to ask it of nine thousand readings, each of which I had sworn to." His breath went out. "So I could afford neither answer: the one would have been turned upon you, and the other upon me. I weighed the pair of them for the better part of a night; and I found that it was the second which kept me from sleeping, and that a better man would have been kept awake by the first."
   142	
   143	"You wrote *declined*."
   144	
   145	"I wrote *declined*, on a form which has no line for why; and I should have written it if there had been no such night. The reason that I gave you at Ostrand was the reason. I do not take it back." He shut his eyes. "But when the pen was down I found that I was glad, and it was not on your account that I was glad. I was relieved that I need never learn what the plate would say. I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one. I have been thought the better of since, by six people on a moor, for the half that I let them see. That is what I have carried from the seat to this bank, and it is not in the flat."
   146	
   147	---
   148	
   149	Cael sat with it.
   150	
   151	He had wanted that answer for four days, since a board at Lowmarch, where he had kept the question behind his teeth and let a small pale woman ask hers. He saw now that he had wanted it for a great deal longer. He had turned it over at an academy, and on a bluff, and across two hundred and eighty days of country; and he had tried mercy in the place where the reason should go, and then policy, and then some long game of the seat's, and every one of them had been a thing done to him or for him. He had been given the true one in a borrowed room, and had never quite believed that it was all. It had not once occurred to him that what lay folded inside it might be a man's relief on his own account.
   152	
   153	It ought to have made the man smaller, and he waited for it to do so.
   154	
   155	It did not. There had been people in his life who had filed him because they were afraid, and each of them had called it procedure to the end. This one had done a right thing, for the reason that he gave. He had then walked three hundred miles on a bad knee and got down on a bank, where he could not rise, to say that he had also been glad of it for a wrong one. Nobody had asked him. Nobody living could have found it out.
   156	
   157	"It kept me off the plate," said Cael.
   158	
   159	"The refusal did, and I shall answer for the refusal, since it was honestly made. I shall not take credit for the gladness."
   160	
   161	"I'm not offering any. I'm saying what happened."
   162	
   163	"Then it is entered," said Vastin, "as what happened."
   164	
   165	The light was nearly gone. Cael looked along the bank at a brown coat and a pair of bound hands, and found that there was a thing in his own keeping which he was going to give this man. It surprised him. He had meant it for the book, and after the book for Lira, who read the book. But he had been paid tonight in a coin that he had not handled before, and he owned exactly one piece of the same mint.
   166	
   167	"You heard her at the fork," he said. "The healer. She told the fire that she'd had her hands on me a long while back, and found a thing, and made me write it down and leave it alone."
   168	
   169	"I heard it. I did not understand it, and I did not ask."
   170	
   171	"It was read again today. Twice. Once behind a hedge at a brook, and once here, against this stone, just before you came along."
   172	
   173	The old head came round a little, and stopped. Cael watched the question arrive. He had seen a great many arrive in that face across a table, each in its order, and he knew the look of this one: it was the obvious next thing, and any officer living would have put it, and put it at once. *By whom?* The healer was two days north, with a mule. There was a boy on a bank who had lately had a hand on his own chest.
   174	
   175	Vastin did not put it. His eyes went back to the moor.
   176	
   177	He had done the same once before, on a bluff, with a notebook lying shut on a table between them and one more question plainly in reach. Cael had taken it then for a thing that the man had missed, and he knew better now: it had been looked at and left where it lay, and tonight it was being left again.
   178	
   179	"I'll not tell you what read it, or how," said Cael. "That part's not only mine."
   180	
   181	"I have not asked."
   182	
   183	"No. I saw you not ask." He drew a breath. "It was read with the one thing I've got that reads, and it came back with nothing. I don't mean that it's empty in there; I mean nothing came back. What I read with can feel a thing that moves, and whatever's in that place doesn't move, so the reading's got no way to say a word about it. I've been writing that nothing down for the better part of a year as if it were an answer. It wasn't one. It was only the thing I read with, telling me where it stops."
   184	
   185	Vastin had gone very still.
   186	
   187	"The instrument is not at fault," he said slowly. "It has told you where its reach ends."
   188	
   189	"That's it. That's near enough what I'd have written."
   190	
   191	"It is a sentence of mine. I have said it at inquiries for forty years, about frames." The left hand opened as far as it could, and shut. "I wrote it once of a person, in an assessment which a certain seat declined to receive, as lying outside its schedule; and I did not suppose that I should hear it said back to me on a roadside by the person."
   192	
   193	"I never saw that paper."
   194	
   195	"No. Nobody did. It was the one honest reading of you that the Compact ever held, and it said that I could not make one."
   196	
   197	"So you'd have had the first of your two," said Cael. "I can't swear it; a plate's not what I've got. But there's nothing in that place that moves, and I think a plate's the same sort of tool as mine. It would have lain flat, and I'd have been two blanks on one file, and they'd have found their word. Nobody has that but you. It's not in the book yet. I've not told her, and she's read every line I ever wrote."
   198	
   199	"Why is it given to me?"
   200	
   201	"Because you gave me the other. I don't know any different way to pay for a thing like that." He stopped. "I'm not making it out to be more than it is."
   202	
   203	"It is not more than it is," said Vastin. "I measured a person and could not read him, and tonight he has told me that he cannot read himself. I have had few friendships, and I know the look of them. This has not that look."
   204	
   205	"No."
   206	
   207	"It is a place where two people can stand. I have not had many of those, either."
   208	
   209	It was not friendship, and Cael did not mistake it for that. A friend was a girl who sat on a bank with her back to a hedge and pulled the heads off grass. This was something flatter and harder, and he thought that it would bear more weight than it looked able to. Two people had each come to the end of an instrument, from opposite sides, and had met there. It was ground.
   210	
   211	"I shall want your arm now," said Vastin. "I told you that I should."
   212	
   213	Cael got up. He went round the post and stood on the slope below the old man, where the footing was, and put out his right arm, bent. A raw hand closed on it above the elbow. It was a good deal stronger than it looked. The stick went into the turf, and the whole long weight of the man came up through that grip in one pull, with a breath shut hard behind the teeth, and stood.
   214	
   215	He did not let go at once.
   216	
   217	"Thank you," he said. He had said it to Brom for a bowl, a dozen times, in the same two words. It did not sound the same.
   218	
   219	Then he took his hand away, and found his balance on the ash, and went back along the bank toward the fire. He went slowly, with the bound leg swinging; and he carried himself, Cael thought, like somebody who had set down the heavier of two things.
   220	
   221	---
   222	
   223	He wrote it by the fire, with the book on his knee and Lira's shoulder a foot from his own.
   224	
   225	He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it, and the place: the three-mile post. Then he put down what he owed, before what he had found, as an old man in Denvash had taught him.
   226	
   227	*Lira asked whether it was the thing I was told to leave alone. I said beside it. I read a healer's order for the leave I wanted in it. I'd do it again, and she's to be told so at the mill, by me, before anything else is said. Round, both times. Not in. Nothing taken up to it.*
   228	
   229	He looked at that, and it was true, and he let it stand.
   230	
   231	The rest came more slowly. He had it in order now. He had said most of it aloud on a bank within the hour, to the last person living whom he would have expected to say it to, and it had been easier to say than it was to write. A thing said to one man in the dusk could be walked away from. He knew what Hesk's book did with a sentence.
   232	
   233	*Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color.*
   234	
   235	He stopped there, with the pencil's point on the leaf, and read the second line from the end.
   236	
   237	He had not known that he was afraid of it until the pencil said so. He had kept that question since he was fourteen, on a great many desks, the wrong way up. Registrars had reached for it, and wardens, and an Archmarshal with a plate; and a healer had found the edge of it with her two hands, on a mountain, and had been made to wait a long while for the rest. Every one of them had come to him, and he had never in his life carried it to anybody. And he was walking to a town, three miles off, to lay it down in front of a man whom he had not seen, on the word of a catalogue.
   238	
   239	He wrote two lines more, since they were owed.
   240	
   241	*V. sat. He told me the rest of why. I told him what the reading can't do, and nothing of what reads. What he said is his, and it isn't entered.*
   242	
   243	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Myself, twice: not counted, as before.*
   244	
   245	He turned the book round on his knee, and held it out.
   246	
   247	Lira took it. She read as she always read, with her lips moving and her chin down, and she went through it twice. Then she laid her thumb on the leaf, beside one line, and did not say the line.
   248	
   249	"You've let me hold your boots," she said. "And your ribs, on a mountain. And a rope with you on the wrong end of it."
   250	
   251	"Not that."
   252	
   253	"No. I know that." She shut the book over her thumb. "I never asked for it. I want that in there as well. I could have, and I'd have got it, and you'd have hated me for a month."
   254	
   255	"A week."
   256	
   257	"A month. I know you." She gave the book back. "Is he going to get it? This man in a hole?"
   258	
   259	"I don't know. I don't know what he reads. I've a line off a catalogue."
   260	
   261	"If he does, I'll not mind. I'd sooner somebody had it who could do something with it. I'd only have sat on it." She pulled her knees up, and looked at the fire and not at him. "And the old man had it before the book did."
   262	
   263	"Not the question. What the reading can't do."
   264	
   265	"I saw him go down on that bank. I've not seen him sit since a hound had his knee. I thought he'd fallen, and then I saw that you weren't moving, and I sat down again." She was quiet for a moment. "Did you pick up what he put down?"
   266	
   267	"No. I put one down beside it."
   268	
   269	"That's two things on a bank."
   270	
   271	"It's what there was."
   272	
   273	Across the fire Brom was showing Karis how a flint was held. She was attending as she attended to a stratum, and striking her own knuckle. Seln stood at the hedge's end. Vastin was on his feet in the gap, with both hands on his stick, looking at nothing; but he was looking at it, Cael thought, as a man looks who has for once no order that he is in the middle of drafting.
   274	
   275	The town lay behind them. He could tell that it was there by the two carters, who sat on the other side of their own fire and faced it, as people face a hearth. There would be lights along the lowest wall by now, and in the gate towers. Higher up there would be one that was pale, and did not flicker.
   276	
   277	He did not turn round. Neither did she. They sat with their backs to it, and their faces to a moor that the six of them had spent the whole day coming down, until the thorn had burned to a red heap and Brom said that somebody ought to sleep.
