   136	
   137	The street ended in an open place, where the ground began to climb.
   138	
   139	There was a trough there, forty feet of hollowed stone with a pipe running into its upper end, and a row of beasts drinking at it with their carters beside them. One of the beasts was a mule. She was small, and brown, and she stood with her rump to the wind and her ears laid out sideways, entirely at her ease.
   140	
   141	Brom had gone half a dozen strides toward her before he stopped.
   142	
   143	He stood in the middle of that open place with a box on his shoulder. He looked at her feet, the near fore and the off, and then at the others.
   144	
   145	"It's not her," said Lira.
   146	
