"That's rotten. It's been rotten a week; I'd not have put a lamb on it." There was a long silence. "I never saw that water give back anything that went in."

"So it's clear," said Brom, from his stone.

Seln had come up onto the bank at the factor's other side. Cael had not seen him climb it. He was wet to the shins still, and he was looking down at a piece of black water the shape of a slate, as he looked at a paper that he had finished with.

"It's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe," said Seln.

The factor turned to look at him, and found nothing to dispute, and came down off the bank.

"Empty's what I posted. I never asked anybody for safe. I've lived on this shoulder since I was born, and that seep'll have its glass back by morning." A hand went into the apron. "There's another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. They'll have to learn it from the beginning, as these did; and by then I'll have had thirty carts of salt up."

The twenty was counted out on the top bar of the gate, in four heaps of five, and a thumb was set to the foot of Pike's slip, where it left a mark like a crook. Cael had been paid at a good many gates by then, and he had learned to read a hold by how it paid. The factor at the axe-head had counted twice, like a man who has been cheated before; this one counted once, and did not look at the heaps afterward, like a person who has got exactly what was wanted and means to think no more about the price.
She stood before Cael at her stride and a half, and she looked at his hands. They lay on Hesk's book.

"You, I've not read. I said at my own plank that when I put my hands on that again I'd know what I was reading for, and I'd have slept. I don't know yet, and I've a round in front of me."

She had begun to push a sleeve up, by old habit. She looked at the hand that was doing it, and drew the sleeve down.

"That's on me," said Oryn. "It's the one thing I'm walking up that road owing, and I'll pay it."

"Then it'll keep," said Cael.

"It'll keep three weeks. I'm not easy about it. I'd sooner you knew that I'm not."

She took up her bowl from the wall's top, and looked into it, and found it empty.
"She'll look for you at every halt," said Brom. He had the mule's nose in his big palm. "She's had three boxes on her since the snow."

"She's a mule. She'll look for where the barley came from." Oryn took up the cheek-strap. "I'll tell her that it's gone under a town."

She did not say goodbye, since she had never said it to anybody, at any gate. She looked at each of them once, in no order, and at a bound knee last. Then she stood a moment before Cael, with her free hand shut, at her own distance.

"Not the steps," she said. "Not the square."

She turned the mule's head to the north.

They watched her go down the rut and past the fold, a square figure in an old coat with a small brown beast beside her. She walked as she walked to any gate, without haste, and she did not look back. Cael had heard why, once, though not from her. A healer who looks back has only doubled what she is carrying.

"It's six bowls," said Lira.
Then he saw what kind of column it was.

Every line of it was about a thing that moved. It told him where the Wind leaned, and how the millstone went by. There was not one line in it about the place. There never had been. *Doesn't flow. Doesn't change.* He had written those on a leaf, and had taken them for findings about the place; and they were not. They were only the instrument's way of saying where it left off.

The going-along felt current, and it had never done anything else. It had been a healer's means of telling how the rivers ran in a body, quick or slow or choked, and it knew a river only by going along with it. Set it where nothing ran, and it did not come back and say *empty*. It did not come back and say *full*, either. It had nothing to go along with, and so it said nothing at all; and for the better part of a year he had been writing that nothing down in pencil, with the day at its head, as though it had been an answer.

He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.

He took his fingers from his wrist, and put his right hand down to push himself up.

It came down on the foot of the gatepost. The going-along had not been called home yet. It went down his arm to the palm, as it had gone to a healer's wrist when he had her leave, and there it stood. The stone gave it nothing to travel on. He had always known that it would not; the tenth did nothing whatever on a dead thing, and he had never troubled to think about that.

What held him there, half risen, with his weight on that hand, was that he knew the feel of it.
"Tonight," he said. "There'll be a halt. They'll shut those gates at dusk, and we'll not be at them by dusk, at this pace."

"At my pace. You may say it."

"At your pace."

"Thank you." He drew a breath, and let it out. "It is a curious thing, to be the slowest in a company. I had never had the opportunity."

The third post came up out of the afternoon, a long way ahead, where the hedges ended and the river meadows began. It was too far off to read; but there was a figure on it, and under the figure three short bars.
"It is the only way to the front door, and a load does not go in at a front door. There is a yard, and a yard has a gate, and carts must reach it by some road which is not a paved square with a station's steps on it." Seln got up off the ladder's head. "I have not found that road. I shall find it tomorrow; and tomorrow at noon somebody will stand at a cook-house counter with a sheet in her hand that a certain man cannot look away from. That is as far as I have got."

Karis laid her pencil down on the flat. Her hand was not quite steady.

Brom turned the lantern low. The blanket hung over the gable, with a faint paleness round its edges that did not flicker; and Cael lay back, with the book shut on his chest and his eyes on the slates a yard above him.

"Last time we walked into a place like this," Cael said, "it took four years and a hearing to walk out."

"Last time we didn't know what we were looking for," Karis said.

"Do we now?"

"A name and a reason," Karis said. "Older than the walls, under our feet, and nobody's read it because nobody's had a reason to look and a person who could read it in the same lifetime." Her hand lay flat on the courier's leather, and it had stopped shaking. "That's us. That's the whole reason it's still buried. We're the first ones who are both."Cael had watched a great many people read. He had watched registrars do it, and magisters, and a small pale woman who went along a line as a hen goes along a row of seed; and this man's eyes did not go along anything. They went to the foot of the sheet first, and stayed there, and then they climbed, as Brom's had climbed a pier on a river bank, from the footing upward, stopping at whatever seemed to be holding weight. He might have been looking at a wall.

It took him no longer than it takes to swallow, and then he laid the spoon down.

"Off floors," Cleon said.

"Fused. Grey. No join. Three of them. I copied every mark on all three. Fifty-two sheets," Karis said.

"Fifty-two sheets," Cleon said.

The eyes had gone back to the sheet. One square pale finger came out, and did not touch it; it moved along above a row of her drawing, an inch off the paper, and stopped.

"Rubbed, or drawn?" His voice was rough, as a hinge is that is seldom used.
He looked at her then, for the first time, as he had looked at the sheet: from the boots up.

---

"Not here," he said.

He had picked up his spoon again, and he spoke between mouthfuls, to the wall, in short pieces; and Cael, by the doorpost, with a bowl going cold in his hand, understood that he was hearing a list that had been ready for a very long time.

"I don't read on a counter. There's gravy on it. That's the first."

"I had not supposed that you would."

"All of them. Not the ones you like. I'll not be shown a choice."

