"It's a very good view. I'd have given everything I had for it. I very nearly did." Her left hand had come out of its pocket, and she looked at that for a moment, the long thin fingers that had been in wool for the better part of half a year, and shut them. "I've carried fifty-two sheets up the inside of it, and seen who fetches its eggs. You can't want a place the same once you've been its porter. I don't think you can, anyway."

She swung the box off the parapet, and got her arms through the beckets, the right and then the left.

"It's worth the look," she said. "I've looked."

"Four hands," she said.

"Four."

"Right."

It was Cael who had given her the figure. She had thrown the count at him across the top of her box, as a crew's count is called at the head of a bad piece of road, and he had answered it before he had taken in what he was agreeing to.

She was into the slot in the wall beyond the parapet before Brom had his own box off the stones; and it was her back that Cael stood looking at, going down between two blind walls, when he understood what he had just been present at.

He had been looking at a town. He ought to have been looking at her. He knew what the top of a thing did to Lira, since he had seen it on the wall of a house above Ostrand, and on a roof there, and on a mountain: the weight going forward onto the balls of her feet, and the chin coming up, and something lit behind her face like a lamp in a room. Everybody who had ever had the raising of her had told her that the head of that ladder was where she belonged; and there was a paper in her coat which said that she had set a foot on it. He went back over the minute at the parapet for any of that, and none of it was there. She had stood flat on both heels, with one hand on a box, and read the place off like a fence that wanted mending. Whatever he had been waiting for on her account, since the rise, had not come. She had not waited for it at all. She had picked up her load.

Karis had seen it. She did not say anything, since she never did when a thing was finished; she only looked after the box, with the flat under her arm, and then stood aside from the slot's mouth.

It was the cut that Seln had drawn in the dust.
"I saw that he did."

"Then what is it?"

She was shaking again. It was not as she had shaken in a hayloft, with a card on her knee. He saw her take hold of it, a breath at a time; and she did not answer him.

It was the desk that answered. Nothing can be said low enough in a place where the air does not move, and Cleon had not opened his eyes.

"Being believed — " He stopped. "I had thought it would be a relief. It's not. It's the end of being able to be wrong."

He had said it to the dark behind his own lids, and he gave no sign of knowing that five people had heard.

Karis had turned round. She was looking at the grey head as a person looks who has heard her own trouble told in a stranger's voice.

Cael had no answer for either of them. He had lain awake over this day, and he had been afraid on her account of one thing only: that a man would look at three floors and shake his head. It had not come into his mind that the man might have something of his own to lose by nodding.
"I'm going up for an hour. Set it on the table's end. Touch nothing." He went by them toward the stair with his eyes already half shut. "There's a jar of water on the shelf. You may have what's in it."

When his steps had gone, Brom walked up the gallery and stood looking at the two chairs, the cushioned and the bare.

"That's thirty years of one man," he said, of the cushion. "It's the shape of him. I'd not fit it."

"It'd not fit you," said Lira. "It's had a lot less to do."

"He cleared the other," said Karis. She had not got up from it. She laid one glove flat on the wiped wood, beside her knee. "He did that last night, after we had gone up. I do not suppose that anybody has sat here in his lifetime."

Brom cut the loaf.

---

Seln was in the loft before them, sitting on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung.
"I had three such, and each was quicker." Seln put his hands into his sleeves. "I told this loft, three nights ago, that I would not have a lie told at a registry's door by anybody who means to go on standing on this side of the Line. I did not then except myself. I found this morning that I had not meant to."

"That's a dear way to be right," said Brom.

"It is the only way that I have found which stays right afterward."

Nobody added to it. Brom went down for the pot, and brought it up on a board, with seven bowls, and set the seventh at the end by itself; and the old man took his standing and thanked him once.

Shadow lifted while they ate, and Cael felt every other sound in him sink; he told it no, and it lay down.

He took out Hesk's book when the bowls had gone, and set the day at its head.

*Two hundred and eighty-fifth day. Treswick; the loft. A hundred and thirty-six. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Lamp, from about the third hour. He says left and I go left.*

He looked at that last line, and let it stand, since it was the first use that anybody had found for him inside a wall. Then he wrote the rest, and it came all in one piece, as it did when he had carried a thing the whole day.
"Vastin. Archmarshal, Compact enforcement. You'll find the name is —"

The clerk's hand had gone to his pen. It was not done on purpose, any more than the other thing had been; a man who has sat at a hatch all his life hears a name and a rank and an office, in their proper order, and his fingers go for the ink. The pen was up, and it was coming over the leaf toward the margin, where a registry writes whatever it is told at its own door.

The porter at the end of the bench had stood up.

Cael had not known him, and he knew him now by the way that he rose, which made no more noise than a coat sliding from a chair. Seln had the cap in his left hand and the frame held by one strap in his right. He was three strides from the ledge, in the plain light of a registry's yard, with a clerk's eyes coming round to him; and he had told five people at a pump that this was the one place in the town where he could not afford to be seen.

It was the clerk that he looked at, and not the old man; and he used the mild voice in which he would have told a carter the hour.

"He's on the count. Four hands, same as yesterday, entered by the practitioner and answered for by her, and the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was. The researcher's at your counter and she'll want to be told we're through," Seln said.

The pen stopped.

It hung over the margin, with a bead of ink gathered at its point, and for the space of three breaths nothing in that corner of the yard moved at all. Behind them a carter was backing a mule to the dock, and telling it under his breath what he thought of it.

The clerk looked at the small grey man who had spoken. He looked at him properly, from the cap to the boots, as he had not looked at any of the four; and Cael watched a face go into a memory that kept things. Then the clerk looked at the old one, who stood with his mouth still a little open upon a word that he had not been let to finish.
He put the wish where he put a cord, and a hair, and a date. He would write it tonight, and he would write that he had not spoken it.

"Left," said the rough voice.

He was there already.

When the bell went they roped the boxes, and Brom took his on the right shoulder again, and nobody said anything about it. On the long stair Cael watched that box for twenty-seven steps, and for the twenty-two after them in the open air; and Brom did not once shift it to the other side, since there was no other side to shift it to.

The dray was gone from the yard when they crossed it. Its two logs lay by the dock, where somebody had thrown them, and the wheel had been found. Brom looked at the place in passing, as a man looks at a piece of work that he has finished and been paid for.

He wrote it in the loft, under the day.

*Brom: the left shoulder wrenched, under a dray in the registry's yard, with no Path opened. He is carrying on the right, in the old healer's knot, against Oryn's word at the fork. I did not tell him to put it down. I couldn't have made him. Owed at the mill, to two healers, by me as much as by him. Entered.*
"What is it?"

"Paper. Not stone."

He took his fingers away, and turned round; and for the first time in four days he looked straight at her, and not at her boots.

"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." His chin went toward the three tables, and his eyes did not leave her. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." The two fingers found the brown tape again, behind him, without his looking for it. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." He let the hand fall from the shelf. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."

Nobody spoke.

He had used her name. He had said, the day before, that he did not forget things; and it seemed that he had kept that one, and had chosen his time to give it back.

Karis had not moved in the chair; her two gloves lay shut in her lap. Cael, at the table's end, could not see her face, and he thought that he ought not to try.

