editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:3:Seln had taken the sheet an hour before, from the foot of the second row, while a small pale woman was reading four words aloud from the foot of another; and it had troubled him a little that it was still so easy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:He had lifted papers out of heaps under their owners' eyes for the better part of his working life. One did not snatch. One was seen to be handling the heap for an honest reason, as he had been, reading six rows for what they left out. One's left hand squared the sheets while the right went on turning them. The sheet was in his sleeve before the pebble was back on its corner. Nobody at the slab had looked up. They were the only six people living from whom he had never before taken anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:7:He stood at the basin now, with his back to the rock, and held the thing low against his body, where the wind could not get at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:So he had stopped. He remembered it without any pride: his hands, and his breath, and the eyes that he had not been quick enough to take off the pencil. The boy had seen. He saw most things, though he was slow to say so. Seln had said that he had read it, which was true, and had gone to a window and looked at nothing for the rest of a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:19:A date read once, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, in another person's pencil, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages to find it otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. So might she, for all that she never did. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were three places where it could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:21:He read it again now, properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:23:He had the sheet itself this time, and daylight, and nobody at his shoulder. He read the technician's date a figure at a time, with his thumb moving under it, as he had not read anything since he was a junior: the year first, and then the day. He turned the sheet over, and looked at the brush's work on its back, and turned it again, and read the date a second time from its last figure to its first. Then he shut his eyes. He set the other date beside it, the one that he had copied twice, and went along the two of them together, as a clerk goes down a copy against its original.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:45:He could take the boy aside. That was better. But it would still be Seln's mouth, and Seln's choice of words, and Seln's face to be read while he chose them. Afterward the boy would have to decide what to tell the others with somebody standing by who already knew. He had been handled so since he was a child. People had found things out about him and then come to tell him what they had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:49:One placed a thing. One did not say it. A paper was left where the right person would come upon it, at a time when he could read it unobserved. He was let to make of it what he would, and was never afterward asked. It was how a man was turned, in the old trade, or warned off, or ruined. It was done so that the subject should believe that the knowledge was his own. Seln had been excellent at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:53:The kindest thing that he knew how to do for the boy was, in every particular that could be seen from outside, the same act as the cruellest thing that he had been trained to do to a stranger. He could find no way round it. The difference lay wholly in what he wanted. He had been taught, by people who knew their business, that what a man wanted could not be seen and ought never to be trusted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:57:That was all. The boy could read a registry date; he had read them upside down on other people's desks for as long as Seln had known him. He knew this one a great deal better than Seln did, since every paper that had ever been served on him began with it. If the sheet lay before him he would see what it was, or he would not look. If he did not look, it was not yet his to carry. If he did, then he might write it or burn it, and tell five people or none. Nobody alive would have the right to ask him what he had made of it. Seln would least of all, having said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:59:It had a cost, and he made himself price it, as that large young man would have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:61:A thing given so cannot be shared. He would never know for certain that it had been read. He could not stand beside the boy in it, or be asked what he thought, or say that he was sorry. He could not be thanked, which he did not mind; he could not be blamed either, and he found, with some surprise, that he minded that. He would be handing over the heaviest paper that he had ever carried, and he would have to do it as a man slides a bowl along a board, and look away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:73:He had not thought of it. He had taken one sheet from the foot of one row, to be sure of a thing before he did anything with it. He had forgotten that she never trusted a count until a second person had made it. She had put that row into the one pair of hands in which a cord had read short. He saw those hands now. They lay flat on the rock, on either side of the heap, and they had gone the colour of the paper. The boy was looking at them as a man looks at a tool that has failed him twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:He crossed the turf, and made no more noise than he ever did. He set the sheet down between those two hands, with its head away from the boy, as every paper had lain before him in his life. He squared it with one finger. Then he went back to the water, and stood with his face to the mule. He counted the sound of the spring, since a man who has placed a thing does not watch it found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:Cael saw that before he saw anything else, and for a breath it steadied him. A clerk who set a paper before a person turned it to face him. This one had been turned the other way, with its printed head toward the far side of the rock, as papers had lain on a registrar's counter at Denvash, and on a magister's desk, and on a table on a bluff. It was how he had learned to read. Somebody had noticed that, and had laid the sheet so on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:He was not one short. That came next, like the blood coming back into a hand. There had been nothing the matter with his counting. A sheet had been out of the row, in Seln's sleeve, and here it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:89:Then he understood that it had not been given back to the row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:91:He looked along the slab. Karis had sat down again on her stone. She had seen the sleeve, and she was writing something very small beside a figure, with her head bent; she did not look at him. Seln was at the basin with his back turned. Nobody else was nearer than Brom, who was frowning at the third row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:93:Cael read the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:95:He had heard it read, by a lamp, in Lira's voice, from the far end of a table. There was the printed head. There were four short lines in a quick hand that leaned to the right, as a man writes on his knee. He knew what the first of them said, and the third, and where the fourth broke off. They had gone round in him for three days, with the stripe that lay across the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:99:He read it as he would have read any other, and went on to the line beneath. Then his eyes came back. He had read a great many such dates, upside down, on a great many desks, and they were all of them much alike; but this one he seemed to have met, as a person seems to have met a face in a crowd.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:101:He did not trust that. He did it as a sum is done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:103:There was one date that he knew as he knew his own name. He set it beside the technician's, in his head, and went along the two of them a figure at a time: the year, and then the day. He did it without moving his lips, with his two hands flat on the rock. He did not believe what he had, and he did it again from the far end, backward, from the last figure to the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:113:He stood very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:115:He had two things. One was a line in a technician's hand, about something under a plate that nobody could open. The other was a date, and the date was his. He could feel the place where a verb would go between them. He could feel a dozen that would have fitted, each of them ready, as the ten were always ready, leaning toward the first stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:117:He had learned from a small pale woman what a verb cost. She had sat up over one in a store behind a chimney, with a cord that read true and a hair in a fold of paper, and had refused to let it finish itself in her own head. He had not understood until now how hard that was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:123:It was true, every word of it. It did not help at all, and he held to it regardless, as he would have held a rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:129:He looked at the blank beneath it for as long as he could have held his breath, and it frightened him more than the date had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:135:He lifted the second row with both hands, and laid the technician's sheet in at its foot, where an office had filed it, and squared the heap upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:137:"It's whole," he said. "The row. It was only out for a while."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:139:"I saw where it was." Karis had not looked up; and then she did, and her pale eyes went over his face once, carefully, as they went over a seal. "You read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:141:"I've read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:143:She had been given those words before, in a store, by another man, about the same sheet. He saw her hear them. She did not ask a second time. She wrote nothing, and tied the tape across the second row with a knot that she pulled very tight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:155:Vastin had stood through the whole of the morning. He was standing now, a yard from the rock, with both hands on the head of his stick and his eyes on that flat of waxed leather. Cael, who had had reason to watch faces that day, saw something gather in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:157:"There are places in that country," Vastin said slowly. He was not speaking to anybody. "They have been measured for six years by an office that told no other office. They are classed, by a mark that the survey's own people carry on their iron. A surveyor who formed a view of them had the space for his view struck off the form. And a return upon the deepest thing that the Compact owns has been laid among their sheets, for want of a shelf, with a brush drawn through it." His voice had quickened, and gone level. "This has to go up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:173:He let that lie for the length of a breath. He was not looking at the man. He was looking at the hand that hung in the air, as he had looked at it three times that morning and said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:185:"A man who reaches for a pen that is not there has told the room what he was. So has one who classes a paper before he has read it, or says *it should be directed*." Seln's mild voice did not alter. "I knew you in a doorway for what you were before you had opened your mouth, and I was not the only person on this side of the Line who could have. There is a town at the end of that road. There is a gate in it, and a man in the gate who has been reading people for his living since before I entered the service. You will not need to tell him your name. You will only need to stand at his counter and begin to arrange it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:191:Vastin was silent. Cael watched him do the thing that he had been told, and it was a strange sight; the man had read rooms for forty years, in an order, giving each object its due, and he now had to look at one without doing anything to it. His right hand came down, and found the stick. His eyes went to the slab.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:195:"A courier's flat, strapped. A calfskin, with four stones on it." He was a long time over each. "A book that belonged to a dead man. An iron stake that has never been driven. Some pebbles, which that young woman has laid out by size." He stopped. "And a wind. I had not noticed until now that there was a wind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:207:She sat with the flat across her knees and her two gloved hands laid on it. Cael had seen her so on the hundred and thirty-third night, with a strip of brass and a calfskin, going past the edge of what she had. She did not look tired. She looked like a person who has added a long column and is afraid to read the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:209:"Six years," Karis said. "They measured all of it. They graded it — the sites are ranked by something, and it's the same something in the kit, and I can't read either one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:213:"Everything I can't read points at the same place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:217:He had said it once before, at a board, as a man reads a place off a waybill. He did not say it so now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:231:It was the first time that the town had been named aloud since a page was opened on a table, and the man who had carried the page was the one to do it. It lay on the moor now among the seven of them, like a thing set down. Cael turned the other name over. It was a short one, and he had never heard it. A man sat under that town with his hands on a language, and had a name, and had been waiting there, it seemed, for somebody to bring him three floors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:233:At the basin a square woman had stopped wringing linen. She stood with a wet cloth in her two fists and her head a little turned. She had heard a station spoken of twice at a board, and had shut her mouth on something each time; she had heard an archive spoken of today. He saw her put the cloth down on the stones, very deliberately, as she put down a thing that she meant to come back to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:3:The fork-stone stood where the ruts parted, a slab of the dark rock as tall as Brom, set on end by people who had left no other mark on that moor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:7:The fold lay a little way down the northern branch, in a dip. It was a drovers' pen, round, of dry stone laid shoulder-high without a grain of mortar, and three parts of it were open to the sky. The fourth had been roofed with slabs. In that corner there was a hearth built into the wall, with a flue, and beside the hearth a stack of cut peat as high as a man's waist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:"You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go, from the hag behind," said Oryn. "It's been the rule here since before the stone was stood up. A fire with a wall round it and a roof on it isn't on a road. I've slept here on thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:11:Brom built it, laying the peats on end as she showed him, and Karis set one point among them without being asked; and the thing caught slowly, with a sour brown smell that Cael had never met. It was the first flame that the seven of them had sat by since a hold on a shoulder, and only the second since the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:15:Seven bowls went round, and the mule had the open part of the pen to herself, where she stood with her rump to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:Oryn ate standing, at her stride and a half, and when her bowl was empty she set it down on the wall's top and pushed neither sleeve up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:23:"That's the head of it, and I'll give you the rest in order, since I've had long enough to arrange it. You're going under a town to read. I've nothing to do down there. I can't read a stone; I'd not know a stratum from a stair. I mend what's put in front of me, and then I walk to the next. So you'll not be thinking that I'm coming, any of you, and I'll not have to watch you not ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:41:"Not for the healer's. For theirs. I filled that column in for a long while before I asked what it was for; I thought that it was like the name. Then there was a week of fever in the low town, and the house was full to the stairs, and I found out." She spoke as she spoke a finding. "We'd so much in us, the six of us together, as I've so much in me tonight, and it had to be spent on somebody. It went down the column from the top. There was a Gold in a bed by the window with a wrenched wrist. He had what he wanted on the first day. Then the Silvers had theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:53:"I'll keep that. It's not shame." She set the spoon down in the bowl. "If I put a name to it, you'd think that it was that house, in that town, and that the next would be kinder. I asked, before I went. They keep the same sheet at the foot of every bed inside the Line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:61:She let them have that for the space of three breaths, and Cael did not need it explained. He had been a sheet with an empty column since his Kindling; and he understood now why a healer who had never asked him for a tier had once looked at his hand, on a mountain, as a person looks at a shut press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:65:She had turned from the fire, and her face was as level as he had ever seen it, and a good deal less tired than it had been at the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:71:"North by that rut at first light. There are five gates up along the hill-foot that I've not seen since before the snow, and one of them has a child in it that I was to have heard a cold term ago. Then down the far water to the ford, and over, and on to the mill from above. It's my own round, walked the wrong way about. The mill has waited all the cold for me, and it'll wait as long again and say that I'm late." Her eyes went to the small pale woman on the meal-sack. "How long does a person want, to read a hole under a town?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:91:"You said a station, at that board. He said it first, by the fire, and then with a bowl turned over. I know stations. There's one on the crown of every city inside the Line, with steps going up to it and a square in front, and I've stood in such a square with a line of children and watched them go up one at a time." She did not push up her sleeves. "It's the one thing in a town that's built to read whatever is brought near it. And I've had my hands on you, a long while back, and I found a thing that I made you write down and leave alone. I've not touched it since. I don't know what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:95:"No. So I don't know what a station does with it. That's the whole of my reason, and it's no better than that. I've no finding. I've a thing that I can't read, and a thing that reads, and I'd not walk any patient of mine past the one with the other in him until I knew." Her voice had not risen. "You go inside, you don't go near the station. Not the steps, not the square. You promise me in that log of yours, because I've seen what the log does to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:Nobody spoke, and Karis had lowered her head. Vastin, in the gap, was looking at the healer with an attention that Cael could not read; he had certified those stations, and he said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:He took the book out of his coat, and opened it on his knee, toward the fire. He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it. Then he wrote what he had been asked for, and it took him a good deal longer than the length of it deserved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:105:"Read it," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:107:He read it to her, aloud, with the peat-smoke in his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:109:*The fork. Oryn north; five holds; routing, not the other verb. Her term, given at the fire, taken in the log because that's the form she'll take it in: inside Treswick I do not go near the station. Not the steps. Not the square. Written here so she can read it, and so I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:113:She did not take it; she bent, with her hands on her knees, and read it where it lay in his, from the day to the last word, with her lips moving. He had written *inside*, and he had not seen that until he heard himself say it. There was no slip any longer between himself and that word; he had put it down in pencil, in the plain hand, as a place where he would be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:"That'll do," said Oryn. She straightened. "I can read that. I couldn't have read a promise."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:127:The thick fingers went round a forearm that was no longer quite as thin as a rail. Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:129:"Fifteen days. It held a hound yesterday, by what I'm told, and it's none the worse. It's slow, and it'll be slow for as long again. They've stone floors where you're going, and stairs. Don't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:137:"That's a foot. Ten days, I said, and it's nine now. The shoulder's another woman's knot, and I can't read through it, and she's the far side of a river. You'll be a month and more from her." She put one finger on the drover's turn, as the old woman had, and took it away. "If it tells you anything, you put down whatever you're carrying. You don't undo that. You find somebody with a bench."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:169:"You'll take it under your arm, with the stick." But she had not said it sharply. She had given him as long as she had given a girl's arm, to the breath, and he had marked it; Cael saw him look after her as she rose, as a man looks who has been handed something across a counter and can find no price on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:179:"You, I've not read. I said at my own plank that when I put my hands on that again I'd know what I was reading for, and I'd have slept. I don't know yet, and I've a round in front of me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:191:"Three weeks, and the mill. And you'll not come to me there with what you think that I ought to hear." Her eyes came up, and went along the six of them, and they were as level as a plank. "Bring me whatever walks out of that archive with you. All of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:193:Cael had a thing in him, since noon, that had walked out of a row of paper and that he had not written down. He heard those three words fall on it; and he said nothing, and was aware of a small grey man at the hearth who said nothing either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:197:The boxes came off the mule on the two hundred and eighty-first day, as soon as there was light to rope by, and the frost was white on the fork-stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:209:Seln made no move toward the third, and nobody looked for him to. He went thirty yards ahead of any column with his hands empty, since a crew does not load the one person in it whose work is to see what is coming; and he had a case on his chest already that he had never yet set down. The third box stood on the frozen turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:There was not a great deal else to be done, since Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them off a landing. The tarred case that held a dead man's cord had come off the mule last of all, and it leaned against the foot of the fork-stone, where Brom had set it. The healer's pannier stayed on the mule, with her linen in it, and a bow hung beside it with its one bolt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:235:They watched her go down the rut and past the fold, a square figure in an old coat with a small brown beast beside her. She walked as she walked to any gate, without haste, and she did not look back. Cael had heard why, once, though not from her. A healer who looks back has only doubled what she is carrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:239:"It's seven," said Brom. "I'll lay hers. Somebody can carry it empty for three weeks, and I'd as soon that it were me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:Nobody needed to be told. A tarred case leaned there by itself. She had a flat under one arm and a hide under the other; three backs had a box apiece; and the man who went in front carried nothing, as he had to. On the northern rut the bend of the moor had that moment taken a healer and a mule out of sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:255:"She did. I told her that I should take it under advisement, and I have." He did not look away. "I waited until she could not be put to the trouble of forbidding it a third time. I shall not pretend to you that it was better than that. If the knee complains of a strap, I shall set the thing down in the road and say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:263:The box rode badly on Cael's shoulders for the first furlong, and then he found the way of it, as he had on a stair with something heavier. At a quarter of a mile he turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:265:He had not meant to; but the road had begun to climb, and from where he stood the whole of the fork lay below him in the early light, with the stone at the parting, and the round pen, and a thread of blue going up from its corner where the peats still smouldered. There was nobody on the northern rut for a long way. Then he found them again, very small, beyond the bend of the moor's edge: a dark upright speck and a lower one. They were not hurrying, and neither had turned its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:267:He looked for as long as it took Brom to get twenty strides ahead of him. Then he shifted the box, and went up after the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:273:It was a long bare swell of the moor, a mile and more from its foot to its head. The ruts went up it aslant, as ruts do when they have been cut by loaded carts, and the wind came over the top of it into their faces. Nobody spoke, since it was a road, and the hold lay on all of them; and there was a box on three backs, and a man on a stick behind, who would not be waited for and could not be left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:279:And there was a day. He had not written that, and he did not go over it. He only knew where it lay, as a person knows without looking where the stair is in his own house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:283:The ruts went over a lip of bare rock and began to go down; and the whole country opened in front of him, as it had opened once from a shelf, on the second day past the Line, when it was the east that he was looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:289:It went up in three steps. A wall ran round the foot of the bluff, grey and long, with a great many roofs behind it. Higher, where the ground steepened, there was a second wall, and the roofs inside that were of slate. The third was at the top, close and pale, about the crown of the rock. It was a day's walk off still, and he could have covered the whole of it with his thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:291:"Three tiers," said Lira, at his shoulder. She had set her box down. "They've built the ladder out of stone. I'd wondered what it would look like from outside."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:293:Nobody else said anything, and Karis had stopped a yard in front of them all. She stood with a calfskin under her arm and the wind pulling at her coat, and looked at a place whose name she had read once on the spine of a catalogue that she was not let to open. Seln was counting gates, with his lips. Brom lowered his box to the rock and rolled his left shoulder, and studied those three walls as he would have studied any wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:297:It stood above the highest roofs, at the very top, where a town of that kind keeps the one building that reads. It was pale, and very small at that distance, and it did not flicker as a fire does. It burned as steadily in the full noon as a lamp burns in a shut room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:301:He had seen that it was there; and he turned his eyes from it as he would have turned them from a hound at forty yards, without haste, and set them on the lowest wall, and kept them there. It cost him a little; and he found that it was a thing which a person could do, and that it would want doing a good many times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:313:She looked at him. Then she shifted the hide, and put her gloved hand into the breast of her coat, and brought out a square of oilcloth no bigger than a man's palm. She opened it on the top of Brom's box, along its own creases, and held down the corner that the wind wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:315:There was one sheet in it, of the Compact's ordinary desk paper, in a square upright hand. It lay the wrong way up to him. A schedule of housings ran down it, as dull as a tally of sacks; and half way down, between two strokes of a pen, there was a title that nobody had ever met, and the name of the town in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:He had kept his hand a hand's width from that page for the whole of a lamp, so that he might still say no to it. He had ruled on a slip instead, at a plank, in daylight. He had walked three days up a road behind a job. Somewhere on that road the no had gone, without his having marked the stride; it had gone at a spring, perhaps, between two hands laid flat on a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:319:He put out his right hand, and laid it flat on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:321:It was only paper, cold, and a little rough, and the wind moved it under his palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:5:It took her three breaths, in which Cael saw her eyes go to five grey shapes, and leave them, since they were past her trade. They went to a girl who was wiping a long knife on a tuft of bents with her left hand; to a small man who had come out of the broken ice wet to the shins and was looking at nothing; to a large one sitting on a stone with his foot out. They came to Cael, and passed over him as they passed over a gatepost. Then they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Two days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:35:"Then stand," said Oryn, "and you'll stand still, and you'll put your weight on the big one's shoulder and not on that stick. I'm not kneeling on a frozen road to have you fall on me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:37:He did as much of that as he could bear to, which was to lay two fingers on Brom's shoulder, as a man touches a banister that he does not mean to use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:She knelt, and slit the frieze with a small knife, from the knee to the boot's top, and laid it back; and she told him what she was going to do before she did it, as she told every person who had ever lain on her plank. There were four holes and a tear, none of them deep, since a stiff leg in a thick coat is a poor mouthful. Those she would close. It would take her a quarter of an hour, and it would feel like cold water poured into the bone. The joint was another matter. It had been wrung like a cloth, and she did not mean to spend on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:47:Her thick fingers went round the leg, above the place and below it, and her eyes shut. Cael had lain under those hands. He knew what was passing along that shin now, slow and exact. Tide, the tenth of his own, lay in him as still as a pool, and did not so much as ask; it knew its owner's work when it was near. He had been handed the reading without the mending, a ruler and no saw. Watching her now, he could have said to a breath where along that shin her attention stood, and he could not have done one thing about what it found there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:49:When she had done, she was grey about the mouth. She had spent on four small holes what a child at some northern gate might want of her within the week, and she had weighed that before ever she knelt, as she weighed every spending; it was why the joint was to have linen and a month, and nothing more. She took linen from the mule's pannier without getting up, and two laths, and bound the knee from above the joint to below it, turn upon turn, until it would bend no more than a post. Then she reached up, and he gave her his wrist, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:65:"As to the last, I cannot undertake—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:81:It was a hold's mule, old and sour, with a sack over its eyes, and the factor took it by the cheek-strap and walked it into the cut at the foot, between the dead carts, on the dry edge. Cael understood that he was watching the only test that such a person trusted. They came up the whole hundred strides, past six people and a healer, with a pair of pale eyes going to each grey shape in turn. They went as far as the black wet rock at the head. They turned there, and came down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:93:Seln had come up onto the bank at the factor's other side. Cael had not seen him climb it. He was wet to the shins still, and he was looking down at a piece of black water the shape of a slate, as he looked at a paper that he had finished with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:99:"Empty's what I posted. I never asked anybody for safe. I've lived on this shoulder since I was born, and that seep'll have its glass back by morning." A hand went into the apron. "There's another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. They'll have to learn it from the beginning, as these did; and by then I'll have had thirty carts of salt up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:The twenty was counted out on the top bar of the gate, in four heaps of five, and a thumb was set to the foot of Pike's slip, where it left a mark like a crook. Cael had been paid at a good many gates by then, and he had learned to read a hold by how it paid. The factor at the axe-head had counted twice, like a man who has been cheated before; this one counted once, and did not look at the heaps afterward, like a person who has got exactly what was wanted and means to think no more about the price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:113:Karis had her notebook open on the gate's bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:115:"I have read of that Path," she said, to the leaf. "There is a description of it in a manual of engagement fields, with a diagram, which I had always supposed to be drawn wrong. I saw it from the foot. It looked as though eight animals had run into the end of the world."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:119:"That a plane stood across a cut, from a line that cracked the ice. That it stood for as long as he did not move, and went when he let it. That eight struck it and none passed." Her pencil went on. "And I have added a sixth line to my five. *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* I had the three wrecks under my eyes from the foot for the better part of an hour, and I did not read them either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:129:There was no rule against a fire under a roof, behind a wall as thick as a cart. Cael sat with his back to it and felt the cold of the cut go out of him a little at a time. Shadow had come up to his knees with the dark, as it did at every dusk, and he held it there; the man whom it had come from sat two places down, and had not spoken since the ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:131:Vastin had not sat at all. There was room on the bench, since Brom had left it, and he stood by the door-post instead, with the stick and the bound leg, and ate what was brought him from a bowl held in his hand, as a man eats at a muster. Lira watched him do it for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:145:He had set his bowl on the sill. He spoke to the fire, and it was plain that he had been arranging the thing since the hound was lifted off his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:151:Cael had read of such floors in a library above Ostrand, in the lists of other people's hearings, and had never thought to ask what stood at the edge of them. It appeared that an officer had stood there, with a stick, for the whole of a working life; and he had been so sure of being the thing in the way that he had never had to learn what a wall was worth when nobody wished to pass it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:171:Vastin received it as he had received the price of a loaf. He bowed his head by a little, toward the bench's end, and it was not the short stiff bow; it was a good deal less practised than that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:He said it without any sourness, as a clerk enters a balance that he does not yet know how to settle. Then he asked the hold's woman, who was passing with a pitcher, where a guest might sleep who was on nobody's sheet, and paid her a copper for the answer, and went out by himself into the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:"He took every word of that," she said. "About the month. He heard it, and he told me that he couldn't undertake it. And he's not knelt since, or gone faster than a walk, or leaned on anything but that stick." Her spoon went round the bowl. "I've had patients who said *yes* to everything and did none of it. I never had one before who said *I cannot undertake*, and then did it to the letter, as if it were a thing that he'd thought of himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:189:The others were down already in the hay, where he could hear Brom turning his foot about to find a way that it would lie. He began as he began every entry, with the day, and with what could be counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:195:He stopped there, with the pencil's point on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:197:He had gone back over it a dozen times since the ice. Eight hounds would have come down to the foot of that cut, and found three quiet things, and stood. They would have tried one of the three with their teeth. He believed that it would have held; a healer and a researcher knew how to be posts, and a mule answered with nothing that a hound hunted. He believed it. He had not been asked to watch it tried, since a man with a stick had put a road's width of refusal in front of it, on his own judgment, with a knee that he knew would not carry him. The rule had been broken, and he had broken a rule himself, twice, for a calf and for a carter. He could not find it in him to enter the breaking as a fault.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:199:He wrote the rest as it came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:201:*Vastin fought. He rooted the road and nearly got eaten for it — a wall's no good if you're the only thing making noise behind it. He knows fields. He doesn't know packs. He'll learn or he won't. Watched an Iron Wall go up two feet from me. Filed it. Note the day: an Archmarshal took a wound out here and there was no one to report it to. He looked almost free. Almost.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:203:He read that over; and the last of it he had seen in the yard, an hour ago, and had not known until he wrote it what he had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:209:*The point: Ember, one grain, at the mark. In earnest. The left palm, though I held nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:211:*Wind: one burst, away, ten strides, to the north bank inside the wall. The lock was spent behind another man's wall. It cost me nothing but the count, and I was more afraid in that count than I have been since a hall.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:223:"It's the same sentence in a clean shirt." The hay rustled. "I was on that lip as well. I looked at three carts for two hours and thought about my arm. Write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:233:On the two hundred and eightieth day they went up through the cut as soon as it was light, on the dry edge, in file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:The seep had been at work in the night, and where a heap of hounds had broken the glass there was a new skin already, clear as a window, with the crack still showing white beneath it in a line from bank to bank. The factor's people had dragged the five away, and nothing else in that place was any different. Cael walked past Brom's stone, and past the spot where a stick had come down, and did not put his boot on the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:239:It was only for a breath, while he stood with the bound leg a little out and looked at a hair's breadth of white under new ice, as a man looks at his own name in a book where he had not expected to find it. Then he came on, a step at a time, with the stick doing what the knee could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:241:"The mule's carried worse than you," said Oryn. She had the cheek-strap, and the three boxes sat on that brown back as they had sat since the landing. "Two of those would shift to the other side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:249:Beyond the cut's head the ruts came out onto the open shoulder, and the whole country changed. It was high moor, bare and brown, rolling away westward in long swells like a sea that had stopped. The wind came over it steadily from the west, with nothing in it but cold, and it had not left off since the first of them topped the rise. There was no tree. Far off to the north a line of blue hills stood up, with snow on them still. Somewhere under that western sky, not two days off, the country that kept no books came to its end; Cael had crossed that edge once, going the other way, by another road, with his name in every ledger behind him, and he had not turned his head to look at it then either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:255:There was an outcrop of the dark plated stone a furlong to the south, as big as a barn, and under its eastern face the ground was green, where a spring came out. It rose in a basin that somebody had built round with stones, a long while ago, and ran over the basin's lip and away down the moor in a thread of rushes. Beside it a single slab of the rock had fallen flat from the face. It lay level, waist-high on its own rubble, twelve feet long and half as broad, with the outcrop standing between it and the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:259:She looked at that slab as Cael had seen her look at a wall of cut courses in a marsh. It was off the road by a furlong; a person might speak there. It was in daylight, and dry. And there was not a bench near it, or a meal-sack, or anybody's elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:265:"It is the table that I have been looking for since the night before last. I said that I should count in daylight. I have carried six years of an office's paper for two days up a road and through a fight, and I have not yet turned a sheet of it." She had the flat out from under her arm already. "I want the forenoon. I want pebbles, a great many, about the size of a hen's egg, since there is a wind. And I want every person here who can read to wash his hands in that basin first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:267:"That knee wants the forenoon as well," said Oryn. "I'd have stopped you at the next water, whether there'd been a table or not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:5:The wind could not reach them there. It went over the top of the outcrop with a steady hollow noise, like breath across the mouth of a jar, and now and then a little of it came round the rock's end and lifted a sheet's edge. The sun stood a hand above the eastern moor. It was a thin white light, with no warmth in it, and it showed every ruled line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:7:"I shall count first," she said. "Nobody is to read me anything until I have counted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:She slipped each tape in turn, and went through the row beneath it by the corners, with her gloved finger and her lips; and she did it twice. She wrote each figure in her notebook under the row's place, and said none of them aloud. It took her the better part of an hour. Nobody hurried her. Lira brought pebbles from the basin's overflow in the lap of her coat, a great many more than were wanted, and set them along the slab's edge in a row, by size.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:11:Cael had washed his hands with the rest, and he stood at the slab's far side, where every sheet lay the wrong way up to him, as paper always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:13:He had not known where else to stand. He had touched neither the cord nor either strip of brass for a hundred and twelve days, by his own ruling, since a thing that measured had read one way in four hands and another in his. Paper was not a cord. A sheet said what it said, whoever turned it. He told himself so, and kept his hands on the rock's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:17:She had unloaded the three boxes herself and turned the mule out on the green. She had looked once at six heaps of an office's paper weighted down with river stones, and her mouth had gone as it went at the smell of a wound that had been left too long. Then she had taken her strip of canvas to the basin, and a cake of grey soap, and begun to wash linen with her back to all of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:21:Brom sat on the basin's rim with his right boot off and his bound toes in the overflow, which was as cold as a knife. He had been told by nobody to do it, and the healer had looked at the foot, and said that it was sense, and gone on wringing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:25:She had gone back to the uppermost row. She went through it a third time, quickly, looking only at the heads; and this time she did not count upon her fingers, as she had by the lamp. She sorted. Where the four signs at a sheet's head were the same four as on the sheet before, it stayed where it lay. Where they were another four, she turned its corner up. She could not read a sign of them. She could tell one group from another as she could tell Brom's face from Lira's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"I said by a lamp that I should give you a number when I had checked it in daylight," she said. "I have checked it, and I am not going to give one, and I owe you the reason. What I counted that night were groups of signs. I let you think that I was counting places, and I thought so myself." She laid her pencil down. "I do not know that. Two clerks may sign one place in two ways. One place may be entered under one group for its floor and under another for its fence. Until somebody can read a sign, a group is a group, and I shall not put my name to how many holes in the ground it comes to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:33:She drew out a sheet from under one turned corner after another, and read down the third column of each with her lips moving. A cord knotted at the fives had been run out from a centre to an edge. She did not need to turn lengths and knots into anything; she needed only to see which entries agreed. It was some time before she laid four sheets side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:37:"And the others?" said Lira. She was looking at the corners that were still turned up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:39:"The others are sheets whose signs I have matched to nothing. I shall not call them places, and I should be glad if nobody else did." Karis turned the corners down again, one after another, and squared the row. "They go back as they came out. When a sign can be read, I shall know what I was holding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:41:Cael looked at the calfskin, where a ruled line went through four rings and off the edge of the skin onto bare rock. He had watched Karis slide a strip of brass along that line, a hand at a time, on the first night of a cold term, past the fourth ring and onto the table, as though the deal might have something to say about where it went next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:45:"Then I shall read for what repeats," said Karis. "It is the only reading that I am good for, with a text whose words are shut. Seln, you will read for what is not there; you have told me a dozen times that it is the half of any paper. And you—" she had turned to the man who stood at the slab's end on his stick, with his bound leg a little out— "will tell me what the form is. I do not know this office. I do not know what a clerk in it is obliged to write, and so I cannot tell what he has chosen to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:47:"I can tell you that," said Vastin. "It is very nearly the only thing that I am still able to do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:49:He had not sat. Oryn had rebound the knee at the basin as soon as they halted, with him standing against the rock, and had said nothing to him about stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the registry's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:55:She went down them, a column at a time, across all six rows, and it took her a long while, and she gave it to them as she found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:57:The measure did not change. That was the first thing, and she read it three times before she would say it. Each of the four places on the hide had been run out from its eight stations in every one of six years, by four different surveyors, to judge by the figures' shapes; and not one radius had altered by a single stride. She had expected that a thing would grow, or shrink. A marsh did, and a river-bed. These had sat where they were as a millstone sits.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:65:The fifth column she had read by a lamp already. There were three clerks' hands in six years: a round one, and a cramped one that leaned backward, and one that made its capitals like gates. Each of them had written the same single word against every station of every place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:71:"He is required to say how his instruments behaved. I suppose that somebody once wondered whether a cord, or a level, would go wrong in such a place as a sigil does. So there is a column. And in it, against every station, for six years, in four hands, there is one entry." She read it out. "*Cord true. Edge true. Level true.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:75:Nobody looked at him. He was grateful for that, and he knew what it cost at least one of them. A surveyor's cord had been carried into each of those places, year upon year, by men whose business was to notice if it read amiss; and it had read true, every time, as it had in an inn yard for Karis and Lira and Brom and Seln. There had never been anything the matter with the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:79:Karis did not lift her eyes from the sheet. She had a hair pressed in the fold of her notebook, and a line under it that said *no inference entered*, and she turned the leaf of the abstract over a little faster than she had turned the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:83:She drew that one out. It was from the fourth row, and it had the Stair's signs at its head, and the number thirty-two. At its foot, in the ruled space, there were four words. They were not in any of the three clerks' hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:85:She did not read them at once. She reached behind her, to the tarred case, and brought out the dead man's ledger, and laid it open beside the sheet; and she looked from the one to the other for as long as it took Brom to draw his foot out of the water. Then she shut the ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:89:She read the words to the whole slab, in the voice that she kept for another person's finding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:91:*Behaves as device. Cannot be.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:95:"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write. He was a man who lived among instruments, whoever he was, and he knew what a device does. It does the same thing at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule." She had laid her pencil across the sheet's foot, to keep the wind from its corner. "That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:"I felt it, at three of those lines. So did four of you. There was no dimming. It went out at one stride, for a third rank and a first and a Copper alike, and came back at the same stride, whole. It was not as a person tires. It was as a row of lamps—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:107:Cael had never seen her stop so. Her mouth stayed a little open on the next word, and he watched her look at it, as she would have looked at a figure that had come out too neatly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:115:"You'd got as far as lamps," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:121:Seln had read all six rows while she was at the fourth column, standing at her shoulder and then at the next heap, and he had not touched a pebble that he did not put back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:133:He drew a sheet from the third row, and one from the second, and laid them beside the Stair's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:"On the form that those four words were written upon, there is a space at the foot for remarks. On the next year's printing there is none. The rule that closed it off has been taken out of the block, and the fifth column runs to the foot of the sheet." His mild voice did not alter. "Somebody read them. It is the only sign in six rows that anybody read anything at all; and what he did, having read them, was to see that no surveyor should have room to write four more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:143:Vastin had listened to the whole of it with his head a little bent, as he might have listened to a junior presenting a file. When he spoke it was to Karis, since it was she who had asked him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:153:He had been looking along the slab while he spoke: at six loose heaps, and their tapes lying in a tangle, and the wind lifting a corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:155:"It should be directed that each row be taken up singly, and re-taped before the next is opened," he said, in a different voice, level and a little quick. "Under two hands. A schedule to be made of every sheet as it is turned, with its row and its place in the row, and the schedule to be signed by—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:165:It was not a quarter of an hour before he did the other thing. She had laid the Stair's sheet back, with the four words at its foot, and he had bent to read them for himself; and Cael, who was watching, saw the grey head come up very sharply.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:181:She had the four sheets side by side again, the three that were alike and the one that was double. She laid her pencil along the top of them. To the right of the four signs and the number, on every one, a small square had been ruled off by the printer, with a heading above it in the same signs that nobody could read; and in the square, in ink, a clerk had set a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:183:It was a short stroke, and beneath the stroke a bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:185:Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of an iron stake that lay in a tarred case, three feet from her elbow, with its point still bright. It was on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:191:"No. And it is nothing to do with size." She laid her finger on the sheet whose measure was double. "This place is twice the others, and it has the same stroke and the same bar as they have, drawn no larger and no differently. Whatever that square is for, it is not for how large a thing is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:199:"I cannot read the heading. I cannot read the mark." Her pencil had stopped. "I had two with it and two without for the whole of a cold term, and I made nothing of them. I have four alike this morning, and I can make no more of four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:She took the dead man's ledger again, and opened it at its first page, and set it down above the four sheets, so that a small pressed stroke and bar in pencil lay over four in a clerk's ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:213:"One thing more, before these are shut," said Karis. "I have a figure for every row, and I made each of them twice; but I made them both, and a count that one person has made is only a hope. I want a second hand on each. Take a row. Count its sheets, by the corners, and say nothing until you have the figure. Then I shall tell you mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:215:She gave the first row to Lira, who read everything with her lips and would count so. The third went to Brom, who dried his hands on his breeches a second time before he would touch it. Seln had gone to the basin, and stood there looking at the water with his back to the slab; she set the fourth by for him, and took the sixth herself, since it was the oldest and the softest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:219:He looked at it. It lay in its loosened tape, a heap of printed sheets as thick as two fingers, and it was the first thing that anybody had put into his hands to be measured since a cord in a yard. He had been waiting all morning to be passed over. He saw now that she had not passed him over, and that she had done it on purpose: a small pale woman had thought about whether to give him a row to count, and had given it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:223:He counted them. It was not hard. He lifted each sheet by its lower corner with one finger, and let it fall upon the last, and said the number in his head; the stock was soft, and it clung a little, and twice he went back three sheets to be sure that he had not lifted two together. He came to the row's foot, and to a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:245:The wind came round the end of the rock and lifted the corner of the uppermost sheet, and let it down. Nobody at the slab moved. Lira had turned her head; Brom had his thick fingers flat on the third row, as if it might be the next to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:247:Cael looked at his own two hands, which lay on either side of a heap of paper. It was a cord that he was thinking of, run out three times across an inn yard, with a slip of ash laid down at its end and a scratch on a stone trough under the middle of the slip. It was a hair, in a gap that had been measured for it. He had been one short then. He had held the thing exactly as four other people held it, and it had come out a piece under what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:257:He had not heard anybody cross the turf. A small dry hand laid a single sheet down on the rock in front of him, between his two hands, and squared it with one finger, and went away again. It was thin stock, and newer than the rest, and it had been folded once. It had a printed head. At its foot a bar of black lay across the paper, as broad as a thumb, cracked like mud in a rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:259:It had not been laid at the foot of the second row, where it belonged, and it had not been given to Karis, who was the keeper of it. It had been set before him. And the man who had set it there did not say one word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:5:The six did the last three miles through river meadows, in a white mist that lay as high as Brom's belt. The road ran on a causeway there, with a ditch on either hand, and the town stood up out of the mist ahead of them like a thing set on a table. Cael kept his eyes on the foot of it. He had done so for a day now, and it had begun to be a habit of his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:She had stopped so short that he all but walked the box into her. She stood on the bank at the bridge's end, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, and she was not looking at the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:21:Brom set his box down. He went and stood beside her, with his head on one side, and studied the pier for some time, as he would have studied any pier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:25:"And the tower. Look where the tower's foot comes down on the bridge's end." Her voice had gone up, and thin. "The courses do not meet. The mason has cut each of his pale stones to lie against the dark, one at a time, as a joiner cuts a shelf to fit a crooked wall. Nobody does that with a thing that he is building at the same time. He does it when the thing was standing there before he came, and he has been told to build up against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:"The bridge was first. The bridge was here when there was no wall for it to lead to." She straightened, and she had gone a little pink, which he had seen perhaps three times. "I can say that. I can say it on the evidence of my own eyes, on a river bank, without a catalogue. It is the first thing that I have been able to say about that town which I did not read on the spine of somebody else's book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:33:"I do not know. I should want to see the bed of the river, and I should want a week." She looked along the five piers, hungrily, as Brom looked at a pot. "The roadway is worn in two grooves, there, and there, and they are deeper than a wheel's rim. I do not know how long a wheel takes to do that to such a stone. I have not got a sheet for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:35:"You've not got a hand for a pencil, either," said Brom. "You've a hide and a flat. Look at it going over. It'll be here on the way out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:39:They went over in file, in the bays and out of them, as the carts came. *What does it carry?* asked the read, of the roadway under his boots, and he told it that it need not know. He kept his eyes on the parapet, which was as high as his hip and capped with long dark stones that a great many hands had polished; and when the parapet ended he looked at the water. A man who is keeping his eyes off the top of a thing learns a good deal about its bottom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:41:The stick came over behind him, a stride at a time. On the third pier it stopped; and when he glanced round, the old man was standing in the bay, with one hand flat on the dark stone and a look that Cael had not seen on him before, which was simple curiosity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:53:It lay across the roadway under the arch, a single long stone, worn hollow in the middle, with a socket cut in each end where a bar had once dropped. He knew what it was before Seln's hand moved. He had stepped over its fellow going the other way, on another road, two hundred and eighty-two days ago, with four others; and he had begun a count that morning which he had kept ever since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:He had come back inside a Compact wall for the first time since he walked down a bluff with a house's certificate in his coat; and he had come as the fifth part of a movement of a stranger's thumb. Nobody had asked what he was. No pen had been lifted. He went on up the paved slope beyond the arch with his box on his shoulders, and his heart going as it had gone on the lip of a cut, and found that he could not have said whether he was relieved or insulted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:81:Vastin walked through the gate of Treswick as the sixth bead on a wire, and nobody stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:85:The street beyond the arch was as wide as a field.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:93:Cael saw them when he had been shown. Each was a slab of black slate as tall as a child, hung beside the step, and each was ruled with chalk into the same columns. He read the nearest upside down by old habit, and then turned his head and read it the right way. *Wanted*, said its head. Under that, a load, or a number of hands. Then a place: *the landing*, or *the second wall*, or once, *the third*. Then a rate. And at the row's end there was a daub of colour, which was blue against the second wall and a pale buff against the third, and against the landing nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:99:"Is the pass that goes with the work. A person without the tier may not live above a wall. But the wall cannot do without him by day. So it lets him in by the piece. He has a slip, with a coloured edge, that says which door he is going to and how many he is; he is counted in at the gate above, with a pen, and he is counted out again before dusk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:101:There was a long bench against the house across the way, and a dozen people sat on it, men and women, with carrying-frames between their knees. While the six watched, a clerk in an apron came out of the next door and rubbed a line off his slate. He chalked another. Four of the dozen got up without a word and went to him, and each was given a slip of card; and Cael saw the blue on its edge as it went into a cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:107:Lira had read four boards while he spoke, with her lips going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:113:They walked in the mechanism after that, close along the house-fronts, and it took no more notice of them than a mill takes of a mouse. It was plain that the street had its own roads within it. Loads went up on the right hand and came down empty on the left. Things on wheels kept the crown of the cobbles. Nobody had painted a line, and nobody crossed one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:117:"That's a walk," he said. "I'll be as long as it takes to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:121:Brom went along the pegs. He passed three-strand without a glance. He stopped at a coil as thick as his thumb, and took a fathom of it off the peg, and bent it double, and looked at the cut end as a person looks into a flower.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:123:"Four," he said. "Round a heart. That'd live in a block."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:125:A woman with tarred hands had come up beside him. He asked her the price of sixty yards. She named it, and it was a fair one, since he nodded as he nodded at a wall that stood plumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:133:He hung the fathom back on its peg, exactly as he had found it. Cael had watched that debt ride in a big man's head since before the snow, through a whole cold term, and over a river that wanted the rope and could not be paid in the wrong kind. It was the first time that he had seen Brom look at it without frowning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:139:There was a trough there, forty feet of hollowed stone with a pipe running into its upper end, and a row of beasts drinking at it with their carters beside them. One of the beasts was a mule. She was small, and brown, and she stood with her rump to the wind and her ears laid out sideways, entirely at her ease.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:157:Cael marked nothing in that at first; a busy smith might well keep two. It was Brom who slowed, and then stood, and then set his box down on its end and folded his arms on the top of it, as a man does who means to be some time. He had spent a good many nights of his boyhood on a heap of sacks in a shed where his father kept a forge for the pleasure of hitting something, and he looked at a fire as Karis looked at a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:163:It had no lad, and no smoke. Its bed was white from edge to edge, with the quiet evenness of a thing that is not being fed; and beside it, well back, stood a woman in a long coat of dark blue with her hands in her apron pockets. She was not doing anything, as far as could be seen. She looked like somebody waiting for a ferry. A second smith worked at that fire, and the horse under his hand was a tall bay with a blue band braided into its forelock, held by a groom in a clean jerkin. Nothing else was waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:171:"It's the same bar," he said. "Look. They're both drawing off the one rack, by the door. Same iron. Same nails; that's one keg between them. He's making the same shoe at both fires, and the white one costs him a woman's wages to keep." He scratched his jaw. "The horse can't tell. A hoof's a hoof."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:195:"The long loft," the woman said, when Brom asked. She had counted them as the keeper had, by the feet. "It's over the horses, so it's warm. A week, the six of you: two marks, in front. That's the pot at dusk as well, a bowl a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:She looked at him as a person looks at a horse that may be going to kick, and then she shrugged with one shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:211:Vastin had stopped in the arch. He stood between the two doors, and his eyes were on the painted one, and on the step, and on the lamp. Cael saw the bound leg begin to turn toward it of its own accord, as a horse turns in at its own gate. Then the old man looked down at the leg, and brought it round, and came in by the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:223:The fifth was a carter, a big slow man with a red beard, and he was not of that hold. He sat at the bench's end, holding his right wrist in his left hand as a person holds a cup that is too full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:243:It was a river with a stone dropped in it. The arm's current came down to the joint, strong enough, a carter's; and at the joint it went round. It ought to have gone through. She followed it, as she would have followed a path round a fallen tree, and found the place: two ends of bone that had been laid a thumb's breadth off one another, and had done what bone does, and joined where they lay. It was good, sound, patient knitting. The body had made the best of what it was given, as the keeper's wife had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:257:She saw to the other four, and ate what the gate-house woman brought out, and lay down in the lean-to beside the mule with her coat over her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:267:And there was a thing that she had not got to at all. She had walked away from it at a fork two days south, with her sleeve half pushed up, and had told a boy that it would keep three weeks. She had read a hundred bodies since she first laid hands on his. She knew a wrist with a stone in it when she met one. She had met something in him a long while back that everything went round, as that carter's current went round a bad join; and she had not been able to say then whether it was a join, or a stone, or a thing that had always lain there. She had told him to leave it be. Then she had walked north, owing him the one reading that might have told her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:275:It took an hour. She spent as she had not spent since a cut on a moor, laying the two ends true and holding them so while the body was coaxed into beginning again; and when she sat back, the yard had gone grey at its edges and somebody was holding a cup against her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:9:Each was no wider than a door. Each ran straight, from the lane, away to his left, between blind walls; and at the far end of each, a hundred yards off, there stood an upright strip of white. It was the same white that had lain on the setts at the head of the street. The square was at the end of every one of them. It lay along that side of the lane, the whole way, like a lit room beyond a row of keyholes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:13:It was harder than the rise had been. On the rise the thing had been a thumb's breadth of light at a day's distance, and he had only needed to turn his head. Here it came at him sideways, at the edge of his sight, a bar of brightness that swung across the cobbles in front of his boots at each opening and was gone. He did not need to look. He had only to walk through it. The muscles of his neck had set like a rope with a load on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:She had come up on his left at the gate, and she had stayed there. She was a hand shorter than he was, with a box that stood up above her head; and at every opening she was between it and him for the two strides that it took to pass. She did not look down the alleys. He could see the side of her face, and it was turned to the lane ahead, and her chin was level. She went by each strip of white as she would have gone by a dog that she did not mean to notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:He did not turn his head. He went through the bar of light on the cobbles, and through the shadow that a girl laid over it; and the broom went on behind him, stroke after stroke, and grew faint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:31:"There is a plan of this town, filed at the seat, which I have had unrolled for me on four occasions. It shows three walls, two gates, a square, and the principal street. The registry is drawn as a block with one door." The stick came down. "I do not find upon it any lane by which a load might reach that building without crossing the paving. I should have said, before today, that there was none."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:Karis had said nothing since the gate. She walked nearest the wall, and her right shoulder all but brushed it, and twice Cael saw her tilt her head back and look up its whole height, as she had looked at a bluff from six miles off. She was counting courses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It turned as it fell. The houses ended on the left hand, and the wall on the right dropped to a parapet no higher than his waist, where the crown of the bluff broke off; and for the length of ten yards there was nothing on that side but air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:49:She had stopped. Her box stood on the parapet's top, held there by one becket, and she had said it to the air beyond, in the voice in which she would have read a figure off a slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:71:It was Cael who had given her the figure. She had thrown the count at him across the top of her box, as a crew's count is called at the head of a bad piece of road, and he had answered it before he had taken in what he was agreeing to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:75:He had been looking at a town. He ought to have been looking at her. He knew what the top of a thing did to Lira, since he had seen it on the wall of a house above Ostrand, and on a roof there, and on a mountain: the weight going forward onto the balls of her feet, and the chin coming up, and something lit behind her face like a lamp in a room. Everybody who had ever had the raising of her had told her that the head of that ladder was where she belonged; and there was a paper in her coat which said that she had set a foot on it. He went back over the minute at the parapet for any of that, and none of it was there. She had stood flat on both heels, with one hand on a box, and read the place off like a fence that wanted mending. Whatever he had been waiting for on her account, since the rise, had not come. She had not waited for it at all. She had picked up her load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:77:Karis had seen it. She did not say anything, since she never did when a thing was finished; she only looked after the box, with the flat under her arm, and then stood aside from the slot's mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:81:It opened in the wall of the last building, a slot a stride and a half wide, and it went down. Cael saw the first flight: five steps of dark worn stone, and a landing, and the head of the next. The walls went up on either hand, three times the height of a man, without a door or a window or so much as a drain. The sky was a strip at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:101:The grey eyes rested on him. Then Vastin shifted the case higher on his shoulder, and set the stick's foot on the first tread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:105:He went down as a man goes down who has thought it through. The stick went first. Then the bound leg was swung out and lowered, quite straight, like a plank let down on a rope; and then the sound one bent, and took the whole of him, and followed. He did that for every step. Cael went backward below him, with a hand on either wall and his heels feeling for the treads, and the box dragging at his shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:113:Cael had both heels down before he knew that he had set them. Compression had come up into them of its own accord, as it did when a load was on its way, and his frame creaked like hemp. He told it to wait.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:The load did not come. A raw left hand, which had not opened flat since a ferry's strap, struck the wall and held there; and the old man hung on it, with his shoulder against the stone and his breath gone, a foot above the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:"Seventeen," said Vastin. He got the stick under him again. "I had it as seventeen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:133:He looked at it from the ground upward. It was of the pale stone, mortared, and it went up five storeys, with a row of small barred windows to each. It had no ornament on this side. A registry does not trouble about its back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:They ran the whole length of the wall's foot, under the pale, close-jointed and without a thread of mortar; and where a pale block had been set upon a dark one, the mason had cut it to lie snug, one stone at a time. She did not say what it was. She had said it once already, on a river bank. Her gloved hand went out, and came back, and closed on the flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:Brom lowered his box to the paving and himself beside it. Lira sat on hers. Vastin stood for a moment, measuring the bench with his eye, and the post at its end; and then he set his back to the post and let himself down, with the leg laid out in front of him, and shut his eyes. It was the third time. He had not asked anybody whether he might.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:He had not meant to write until night. But he had a quarter of an hour, and a thing that he had seen at a parapet, and he knew by now what became of the things that he left until night. He set the day at the head, and the place, which was the registry's yard at Treswick; and he looked at that for a moment, since he had never in his life expected to write those four words together of his own will.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:159:It was three short lines in the plain hand, and it was the largest thing that he had entered since a fork. He had half believed, at a board in Lowmarch, that the first blue coat would have her, and that he would lose her to a keeper reading her name aloud. He had said nothing of it, to her or to the book, since a person does not enter a fear about a friend where the friend will read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:171:Somewhere above him, on the far side of five storeys of pale stone, there was a square. He had come round it by a lane with four keyholes, and down a slot, and he sat now with its whole registry between himself and it, on the low side, among the firewood. He had not set a foot on its paving. He had not lifted his eyes to what stood over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:183:He was a man of no particular age, in a grey coat with ink on its right cuff and none upon the left. Cael did not learn his name that day, or on any day after it. He had the card propped against his inkwell, and he did not look at the bench at all. He looked past it, at what stood on the paving. There were three boxes, which he counted with his eyes, and wrote. There were a tarred case, and a courier's flat, and a calfskin, and he wrote those. He did not ask what was in any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:189:The pen moved to the right, into a narrow column, and made a single mark there. Then it went to a narrower one, at the very edge of the leaf, and waited; and the clerk lifted his eyes to the bench for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not lift them far. They came along the paving at the height of a boot, as a grey keeper's had at a gate. Cael had been read by registrars since he was fourteen, and had learned to meet each of them in the eye, so that it should cost them something. He looked down at his own toes. He had not known that it would be so hard. It was the only thing that he had ever had to set against a pen; and he gave it up, and was a pair of boots among four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:The shutter came down. A key turned, and the low door beside the hatch opened inward, and the clerk stood on its sill with the card in his fingers and the green book shut under his arm. He blinked once at the daylight, as people do who are kept indoors. Then, having finished with the loads, he looked at what had carried them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:207:Cael saw it from under his brows, and saw the breath taken. A line that has been blotted can still be struck through; and a name said aloud on that sill would be a name said at a registry's door, with a yard-man going by. At the edge of his sight a raw hand closed on the head of an ash stick until the linen round it creaked. Vastin was looking at the ground. He was doing it a little better than he had done it at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:211:He looked down at the card instead, at the seal in its corner, which was old, and had a piece gone; and then at the green cloth under his arm. Something went over his face, and Cael could not have sworn to what it was. It was not suspicion, and it was not much like curiosity either. If he had been made to put a word to it, he would have said that a man who had kept a book with one name in it, for a great many years, had at last been given a second line to write; that he had written it, and blotted it, and it was clean; and that he did not mean to spoil it now by learning anything which he would then be obliged to set down underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:217:Cael got his arms through the beckets. He went past a clerk of the oldest registry on the Line, into its foundations, as the quarter part of a figure at the edge of a leaf; and the man did not look at him as he went by. He was looking at the shut green book, with something very like satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:It was a short flight, of the pale stone, and it brought them into a vault that ran away under the building for farther than the lamps showed. There were lamps: iron ones, in brackets, a long way apart. There were pillars as thick as Brom, and between them stacks of bound paper tied in tape, and broken stools, and a dead clock. A registry had been putting things down here for a long time that it did not want and could not burn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:229:The clerk had come down behind them with a lantern. He did not lead; he only walked at the rear, as a man does who is seeing a load to its place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:233:It ended at a pillar, as cleanly as a floor ends at a step. Beyond it the vault's walls were dark, and close-jointed, and there was no mortar in them at all. The air changed in the same stride. It had been cellar air, damp, with mice in it. This was dry, and very cold, and it had no smell that he could name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:237:She had taken her right glove off, without setting down the hide, and Cael did not know how. Her bare palm was flat against the dark wall. She walked so, the whole way down that last stretch, with her fingers trailing over joint after joint, like a person who has been told of a thing all her life and is at last allowed to touch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:241:He had expected something great. It was not. It was low, lower than the round arch had been, and broad, and it was made of plates of black iron laid one over another like slates. There was not a lock on it that he could see, or a handle. It stood in a frame of the dark stone; and on either side of that frame the registry's pale masonry came up, and stopped, each block cut to lie against the dark, as a tower had been cut to a bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:It did not ask as it asked at a gate. It asked once, very low, and he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:267:Above him lay a registry, and above the registry a square. Three walls stood round that, and two gates, with a pen at each; and at the very top of it all, where he had not once lifted his eyes, there was a thing that had been built to read whatever was brought near it. Not one stone of the whole reached lower than this door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:It was Seln who took it off. He had gone down the western side of the rise thirty yards ahead of the boxes, as he went ahead of everything; and at the foot, where the ruts ran in between two banks of thorn, he stopped and let the file come up to him. He had not done that on any road since the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:13:"The hedge does. A hound does not lie up where a plough goes, and a thing that hunts what a Path gives off wants a mile of nothing round its den." Seln nodded at the bank. It had been laid within the year, with the stems cut half through and bent over and woven between stakes. "Somebody tends this. That town works these fields to the foot of the moor, and it will keep a watch that rides as far as its fields go. There will be posts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:31:They went on between the hedges, and for half a mile nobody used the leave that he had been given. Cael had a great deal to say and no notion of how a person began. He had not been silent all those days; they had talked at every fire, behind every wall, and through a whole cold term in a back room. It was only on a road that the mouth was shut, with the sigils in their pockets and his ten lying closed, and he had left off noticing it as a carter leaves off noticing his own wheels. He noticed it now, as it lifted. It was like setting down the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:33:The road found its tongue sooner than they did. A cart came out of a field gate ahead of them, piled with dung and steaming, and the man at the horse's head was whistling. He whistled the same four notes over and over, badly, and did not leave off when he saw six strangers under loads. He looked at the boxes, and at the beckets, and pulled his horse over to let the weight go by, as one carrier does for another. He had given Cael the same length of glance that he gave the hedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:The first post stood where a lane came in from the north. It was a dressed stone as high as Cael's waist, squared, with a figure cut deep in its eastern face, and under the figure three short bars, one above another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:49:*What does it weigh?* asked the read, civilly, of the post, as it had asked of every rut for three days. Cael told it that it need not know. Brom lowered his box to the verge, and rolled the left shoulder, and looked at his right boot as a man looks at a debtor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:51:Lira had her box off before him. She set it down on the grass and sat on it; and she sat with her back to the west.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:57:Nobody had asked it of her. A healer had set a term at a fire, for one person, and had made him write it; and a girl on a meal-sack had heard, and had taken a share of it without a word, as she would have taken one end of a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:65:"Then they'll be flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:71:She walked with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had since a landing, and her eyes had not left the bluff since it came up over the hedges. She was not looking at its walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:81:"No. Every account of them is careful to say so in its first line. He reads how a thing is built. He does not read what it says."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:85:"It is the half that I have not got." Her voice had gone thin, as it did when she came near the edge of what she had. "I have dated three floors past anything that I can lay paper beside. I have forty-one kinds of sign in one order. And I cannot tell you which of them is holding the others up. I do not need a man to tell me what it says, Cael. I shall do that, if it can be done. I need one who can tell me how it stands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:93:There was a station on that crown. Oryn had told him what such a thing was, at a fire, in the plainest words that she owned: it was the one building in a town that was made to read whatever was brought near it. He had stood on the plate of one, once, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and written a word. He had a term in Hesk's book about this one, and he meant to keep it. But the term kept him off a square. It did not tell him what he was keeping off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:95:He did not know. There was a place under his breastbone where nothing moved. It had been read three times, on the boards of a loft and at the edge of a marsh, and not once since a square woman stood over him in the grey of a morning and gave him seven words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:101:He had kept that order as he kept few things, and he looked at it now as he had learned to look at a slip on a board: for what it said, and not for what he wished that it said. She had said *touching*. She had said what she meant by it, too, standing over him: that a person who has found a place in himself goes back to it every night with his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:He was honest enough to see what he was about. He had an order from the one person whose orders he kept, and he was reading it for the leave that he wanted in it, with its author three weeks and a moor away from telling him that he had read it wrong. A registrar would have been proud of him. He would write that down as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:109:But he was walking toward a thing that read, with a thing in him that had never been read; and the healer who owed him that reading had said herself that she did not know what the one would do to the other. He did not see how a person kept a promise about a place sensibly without once looking at what he was keeping away from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:117:"I'm going to read. Myself. As she showed me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:133:"And I'll sit on the bank. If Brom comes round that hedge and finds you flat on your back with a hand on your chest, he'll have you over his shoulder before you can open your eyes, box and all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:137:The second post stood beside a brook, where a plank bridge had been thrown over for people on foot and the carts went through the water. It said six. They had done three miles in an hour and a half, and it was a stick that had set the pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:141:Vastin did not sit. He put his back against the post and both hands on the head of his stick, and stood with the bound leg thrust out before him, as he had stood at every halt for two days. A man in that binding could get himself down to the ground. It was the getting up again that could not be done without another person's arm; and Cael had watched him measure that at a fold, and at a fork, and at the top of a rise, and choose his feet every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:There was a gate into the field behind the hedge, hung on a post of the same dressed stone as the mile-mark. Cael lifted its latch. He set his box down inside, on a headland where the grass had not been ploughed, and lay flat in the lee of the bank with his boots toward the brook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:147:He laid his right palm on his breastbone. He put the first two fingers of his left hand on the inside of his right wrist, where the healer put hers; and he let his breath go long, as a sleeper's does, and counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:151:He went on beyond ten, into the deep reading. They came up out of it one at a time, as they always had, like the furniture of a room when the eyes have grown used to the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:153:Wind was first, since it was the oldest, and it had always leaned toward a girl. It was a dry hiss in his feet, like pale grass going over in a breath of air. Two days ago, in a cut, it had carried him ten strides to a bank, away from the people whom he was guarding; and he had paid for those at the end of them, as he paid for every burst, with a locked beat in which he could not have lifted a heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:155:The read went round beneath it, under his soles, low and even: a millstone heard through a floor. That one was Brom's, and it worked for its keep. It would tell him the weight of anything within a pace and a half, and which way the weight was going; and if he leaned on it long it took his near sight for an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:157:Something ticked in his right hand, as a lamp-wick ticks when the oil is poor. That was Ember, a single point in a single place, and the heat of it came home to the hand that held the thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:161:Compression made no sound of its own. He found it, as he would have found a rope in a dark barn, by leaning on it: his whole frame creaked, very faintly, like hemp under a load. It wanted both heels down, and ground worth sending a load to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:163:There was the held note, thin and steady, like a nail that has been driven and has not quite done ringing. Anchor had asked at every sill and gate since Ostrand whether he would not like something set across. He had told it yes twice since the Line, and no more times than he could count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:165:Higher than that, in his right arm, a wet finger went round the rim of a glass. Blade would put an edge on steel for as long as a struck match burns, and it was the steel that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:167:Shadow he knew by what was missing. By day Shadow lay shut under its seal, and it made no sound whatever; he could tell where it was only as a person tells where a rug lies in a dark room, because the floor does not ring there. It would lift at dusk, as it lifted every dusk. He would tell it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:And the tenth was the thing that he was going along with. It had no sound. He knew it as a walker knows his own stride. It had been a healer's, and it read whatever flowed in a living body under his hand, his own or another's. For another's the rule stood over it in two people's writing: *ask first, every time*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:That made ten. He had called them fragments since he was fourteen, and pieces, and entries in a list. Lying in the grass he had them as the going-along had them, which was none of those. They were ten currents, and not one of them had a bank. Each ran on into the next as water runs into water, and a healer had found that out before he did, with both her hands, and had told him so on a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:179:He did not go at it. He had gone at it three times, and it had cost him an order. He chose one current, the hiss of Wind, and gave the going-along to it as a leaf is given to a stream. He did not steer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:181:A long way short of the place, the Wind began to lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:183:It was farther out than he had ever marked, since on every other night he had been looking ahead, at the place itself. There was no check in the lean, and nothing piled. The Wind bore off as a road bears off round the shoulder of a hill, which has gone that way for as long as there has been a road. He leaned with it. He went by on the one hand, and did not look in; the lean eased, a long way beyond, and the hiss ran on toward his feet as though nothing had ever lain in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:185:He came back and took the millstone, and it went by on the other hand. He took the wick. He took the bellows, which lived closest of the nine, and bent least far and most sharply. He took every one that had a sound, in its turn, and none of them touched the place or slowed for it. Then he let the going-along go by itself, with nothing to ride on, the whole way round, as a man walks the bounds of a field, until it stood again where it had set out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:189:He lay and counted what he had, and it was a good deal. He knew where the place was. He knew how far out the leaning began, for each of the nine. He knew that the way round was worn smooth, with never a ripple in it; and he knew now how far it was about. He went down that column with something like pride, as a small pale woman went down a row that agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:193:Every line of it was about a thing that moved. It told him where the Wind leaned, and how the millstone went by. There was not one line in it about the place. There never had been. *Doesn't flow. Doesn't change.* He had written those on a leaf, and had taken them for findings about the place; and they were not. They were only the instrument's way of saying where it left off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:195:The going-along felt current, and it had never done anything else. It had been a healer's means of telling how the rivers ran in a body, quick or slow or choked, and it knew a river only by going along with it. Set it where nothing ran, and it did not come back and say *empty*. It did not come back and say *full*, either. It had nothing to go along with, and so it said nothing at all; and for the better part of a year he had been writing that nothing down in pencil, with the day at its head, as though it had been an answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:201:It came down on the foot of the gatepost. The going-along had not been called home yet. It went down his arm to the palm, as it had gone to a healer's wrist when he had her leave, and there it stood. The stone gave it nothing to travel on. He had always known that it would not; the tenth did nothing whatever on a dead thing, and he had never troubled to think about that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:209:He called the going-along home, and it came, and lay down. He was cold through, as he always was after it, and as empty as if he had hauled on a rope all morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:213:He knew of two. One stood on the crown of the town ahead of him. It had been built to read whatever was brought near it, and he had written in pencil that he would not go within a square's width of the thing. The other sat underneath the same town, with a short strange name, and read how things were built. Whether a man who could tell how a wall stood could tell how a boy did, Cael had no idea.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:221:"You were as long as Brom's boot," she said. "Did you go in?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:227:"It'll be in the book tonight. You can read it there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:235:The road ran straighter from the brook, between hedges that were older and thicker, and it began to be a town's road. There were carts in both directions. At five miles there was a farm that stood close on the verge, with a woman beating a rug in its yard; at four there was a lane with a signboard at its mouth, and a painted hand on the board, and Seln read whatever stood under the hand without breaking his stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:237:The town came up with every furlong. He kept his eyes on its foot, and he learned the foot of it very well. The lowest wall ran clear round the bluff, as far as he could follow it, with square towers at even distances, and he counted eleven of those before the river hid the rest. There was a gate in it with a tower on either hand. A bridge went up to the gate, long and low, and there were things crawling on the bridge that he took for wagons.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:239:He did not raise his eyes above the second wall. He knew where the third was, and what stood within it, as a person knows where a dog is lying in a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:257:"He was gone for some time. He came back the colour of that road, and a young woman who had been set to keep the hedge asked him three questions and was content with half an answer." The stick came down. "I say so in order that you may know it was seen. I have found that people on this side of the Line prefer to be told. I do not ask what it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:269:"I shall want a quarter of an hour of you before that town," said Vastin. "Not on the road. Somewhere that a man can stop. I have carried a thing three hundred miles that is not in any flat, and I find that I should like to set it down before I go through a gate behind you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:281:The third post came up out of the afternoon, a long way ahead, where the hedges ended and the river meadows began. It was too far off to read; but there was a figure on it, and under the figure three short bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:She had walked nine miles along the hill-foot since noon on legs that had wanted a bed. She had told a carter that she did not put her hands to hard work tired; and she had then spent an hour inside his wrist and set out on a road. It was not the first time that she had broken that rule. It was the first time in a long while that she had broken it before the day's second gate, with the gate still in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:17:She heard him on the bench, by the gate-house lantern, with two fingers on a wrist no thicker than a spoon and her ear against his back. He would not keep still, and she did not need him to. The river in him ran quick and clean. The whistle had gone down into a small low hum, a long way off, like a kettle in another room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:"He's mended himself," she said. "They do, at that age, as often as not. Then I'm sent for to be told so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:27:That hold kept its keeper outside the wall. There was a room built against the wall's outer face, on the road side, with one window and a bed in it, since somebody must sleep on the wrong side of a bar to hear who knocks at night. An old woman stood in its doorway with her sleeves rolled and her hands held away from her apron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:53:She had felt it a hundred times, on a hundred benches, and it had never yet become an ordinary thing. There was the woman's, wide and driven, like a millrace with a week of rain behind it. And inside that, in a bed that was no bigger than a loaf, there ran a second, as quick as a bird's, twice as quick as anything grown, going its own way entirely. It did not know that anything was the matter. It lay across the current of its mother like a boat that has swung broadside in a stream.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:57:Her hands asked. The Path eased what they asked of, a little, so that a wall which had been set like a fist went slack under her palms for the length of a breath; and in that breath she walked the small boat round by the width of a finger. Then the pain came back, and the wall shut, and she stood with her hands exactly where they were and waited for it to pass over. She did that forty times, perhaps, and lost the count, which she never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:61:Both times she stopped as she was, with nothing moving in the room but a tallow flame, and gave it what she had been keeping back for her own walk to the next gate. Both times it picked up, and ran on, and she breathed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:67:Oryn heard the cry from the bench outside, where they had carried her a cup. She had not been able to lift the cup. She sat with her back against field-stone and watched the hill go from black to grey, and listened to a person who had been in the world for less than a minute telling it what she thought.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:71:She had met one body in which ten ran together with no bank to any of them. In the middle of the ten there had been a place where nothing ran at all, and everything went round. She had called it a stone, in her own head, on a carter's wrist, and she had called it a join. Sitting here with her hands as heavy as wet sacks, she knew that it had been neither of those, and that she had only reached for the two nearest words. She did not have a word. She had a child, an hour old, that had lain turned the wrong way in the dark and could not have told anybody so; and she had a boy three days south who was walking into a town with a thing in him that he could not read. She had not read it. She had sent him in with a rule about a square, since a rule had been the only thing that she owned to give him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:Brom had roped them again in the night, for a stair. Each had a second becket now, low down, so that it would sit high between the shoulders and leave a person's eyes clear for his feet. He went along the three of them once more in the half light, a knot at a time, as he had gone along them at a ferry's shed. Oryn's empty bowl was wedged under the lashing of his own, mouth down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other. She had the card inside her left glove, against the palm, and she had looked to see that it was there four times since the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:"You are one short of a figure, and I am standing here. I have carried that case for three days against a healer's express instruction, and I do not propose to hand it to somebody else at the foot of a hill because the carrying has acquired a name." He bent, hooked the strap with the stick's head, as he had at a stone, and brought it to his shoulder. "I have been counted once already in this town, by a man with beads. I found that it could be survived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:125:"Then I shall look at my boots, as a hand does, and somebody with more wit will answer him." The old mouth moved. "I am aware of what I am being entered as. I have signed a great many sheets with that column on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:141:Cael put his boot on the first of the slanted setts with a box on his back and a card going before him in a small pale woman's glove. He marked it, as he had marked a sill, since two days ago he had stood at that kerb and not stepped. It was the same stone; he had only become a different sort of thing to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:143:The blue coats were waiting, as they waited for everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:145:Karis gave up the card. She did it well, he thought: she did not offer it, or explain it, but only held it out and looked at the wall. The keeper with empty hands took it. He looked for the figure, as he had looked for a hundred figures since the gate opened. Then his eyes went back up the card, to its head, and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:159:It did not stay for long. It was perhaps the length of a breath. But the keeper who held it read people for his bread, and he had in front of him a porter with a stick, and a bound leg, and the lightest load of the four, who stood up under it a great deal too straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:163:He was doing it badly. Cael could see that he was. A person who has looked at his boots all his life lets his head hang, and this one held his at an angle, with care, as a thing that he had been told the trick of; but his right hand was shut on the ash, and it did not move an inch toward his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:173:Vastin went under the arch with the case on his shoulder and his eyes on the setts. It was a gate that he had ridden through on four occasions, with a keeper standing at each jamb and the board held out to him, open, so that he might see for himself how it was kept; and he went through it now as one stroke in four, on the wrong side of the pen, and Cael heard him let his breath go when the shadow of the arch was behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:177:Cael had her back in front of him, and a box on it, and above the box the back of her head. He could not see her face. He saw what her head did. As she came level with the board that hung from the second keeper's neck, where a line stood for every stranger who had walked through that gate today on his own paper, her chin turned toward it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:189:The roofs were slate, every one. The street had a gutter down each side of it, with water running, and no dung. The windows had glass in them. At a corner there was a cistern with a brass spout, and a girl was filling a jug at it; and she did not put a copper in any dish, since there was no dish. A row of children's voices came out of an upper window, chanting something all together, and he caught the shape of it before the words: it was a list, and it went up by steps, and at the top of each step there was a colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:193:It was not as the outer street had not looked, where everybody was carrying and a box was only a thing in the way. People here were not carrying anything. They walked with their hands free, in good cloth, at their own pace; and their eyes went over four backs under loads as eyes go over a row of posts. A woman drew her skirt aside for Brom without breaking her talk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:195:Karis had not looked at a single slate. She walked with her face turned to the house-fronts on the upper side, where the street had been cut into the hill and the footings of the houses stood on the bluff's own rock; and twice she put out two gloved fingers, in passing, and drew them along a bed of pale stone at the height of her hip, as another person might have touched a dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:The street climbed harder past the cistern. It had been laid in long shallow steps there, a stride to each, with a raised kerb for the grip of hooves. Behind him the stick had begun to come down out of time. Cael did not turn round; he slowed, by a little, as one slows a line for the last on it, and heard the breath at his shoulder catch up, and shorten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:209:He would have gone by it. It was Lira who slowed, in front of him, with her head turned; and he read what she was reading. The board was black slate, ruled in chalk, as every board in that town was, but it had no loads on it: it had hours, and against each hour a colour. *First bell: buff. Second bell: blue.* And at the foot, in smaller letters: *Cards, after the fourth, at the side door, as the house's means allow.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:"It is the standing order for a district house," said Vastin. He had come up level, and stopped, with both hands on the stick, and he was looking at the board as he had looked at a bridge with five piers. "I have read it in the code, where it is the ninth article of a schedule. I had never seen it in chalk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:"Does it read different in chalk?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:He read the card twice. He read it as Karis read a seal, from the stamp inward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:"Lower archive," he said. He had a dry, exact voice. "I had heard that there was such a card. My father kept this gate before me, and he told me that he had passed one, once, when he was young." He looked up, at three boxes, and at a tarred case, and for rather longer at a courier's flat of waxed leather with a stamp across its face. "That is a good deal of matter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:251:*There?* asked Anchor. It asked very softly here, as a person lowers his voice in an old room. Cael told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:255:He had his eyes on the ground. He had put them there under the arch, before the light changed, and he held them there now as he would have held a rope. He saw setts, small ones, laid in a pattern of fans. They ran away in front of him between two rows of very old doorsteps. A long way up that street, eighty yards perhaps, they stopped being grey, and went white, where the sun came down upon something wide and open and paved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:259:It was a hundred paces off. He could have walked to it while a person counted to sixty. Hesk's book lay against his ribs, under the box's strap, with two sentences in it that a square woman had read with her lips moving; and he was glad, for the first time, that she had made him write them. A thing that he had only said would have been arguing with him by now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:263:A small quick shape came up on his left side as he did it, with a box on its back, and settled there, between him and the street's end. She did not look that way herself. She had her eyes on the lane, and her chin level, and she said nothing whatever; she only walked where a wall would have been, if anybody had thought to build him one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:"You will rope three boxes for a stair, as though you knew that there was one, and Brom will keep off that foot. At noon there will be two of you at the cook-house, and no more." He turned his eyes on Karis, and then on Cael. "One at the counter, who has something to show; and one in the wood-yard, with a bowl in his hand, who is nobody. The yard is where porters are fed, through a hatch, and its door stands open to the room for the heat, so that a person may hear from there and need not be seen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:25:Cael had. He had lain with a strange short word going round in him until the lantern was out, and then he had got up, and found a grey man sitting on a rung in the dark, as he had known that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:53:He stood at the very end of it, by the yard door, where the draught was, with his back to the room and his face to the stone. He was not tall. He had grey hair, cut short and badly, as a person cuts his own; and a coat that had been brown and was now the colour of dust, with dust worked into every seam of it, paler than any that the street could have put there. His shoulders were round; and there was a bowl in front of him, from which he was eating steadily, with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:Cael looked at that for some time. The man did not blink them shut and open them, as someone does who is tired. He kept them closed, and found the bowl with his spoon by habit, and chewed, and rested them as a carter rests a horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:57:His hands were square, with the nails pared to the quick, and they were as pale as roots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:61:Nobody stood next to him. There was room for two at that end of the counter, and the clerks had left it, as the carters on a bench had made room for a keeper; but this was the other kind of room, and Cael knew it well. It was the space that people leave about a person whom they have long since stopped trying to talk to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:65:She was very small in that doorway, with the light behind her, and a roll of oilcloth held against her chest. He saw her look down the room and find the dusty coat. She did not hurry. She came along behind the clerks with her chin level, as she had once walked up to a broker's plank with a hide; and she stopped at the counter's end, in the place that nobody took.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:71:It was the paper that he heard, Cael thought, or the breath of it settling. The grey head turned a little, and the eyes opened; and they were not as Cael had expected, since they were red at the rims, and wet, like the eyes of a man who has been all night over a small flame. They went to the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:73:He did not read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:Cael had watched a great many people read. He had watched registrars do it, and magisters, and a small pale woman who went along a line as a hen goes along a row of seed; and this man's eyes did not go along anything. They went to the foot of the sheet first, and stayed there, and then they climbed, as Brom's had climbed a pier on a river bank, from the footing upward, stopping at whatever seemed to be holding weight. He might have been looking at a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:81:"Fused. Grey. No join. Three of them. I copied every mark on all three. Fifty-two sheets," Karis said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:85:The eyes had gone back to the sheet. One square pale finger came out, and did not touch it; it moved along above a row of her drawing, an inch off the paper, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:87:"Rubbed, or drawn?" His voice was rough, as a hinge is that is seldom used.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:115:"And you want me to say what's written," he said at last. There was a great weariness in it. "They come every few years, with a rubbing off a lintel. They want it read to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:125:He looked at her then, for the first time, as he had looked at the sheet: from the boots up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:"I don't read on a counter. There's gravy on it. That's the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:"They come down to me. I don't come up. I've come up as far as this bowl at noon since before those hinges were hung, and it's as far as I come." He jerked his head, without looking, toward the gate. "What is there to carry?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:147:"Then it's a stair's worth." He felt in the dusty coat, and brought out a square of stiff card, the size of a man's palm, with a buff edge and a seal pressed into one corner, old, and rubbed nearly flat. He reached across the counter without asking, and took the pen that the cook kept for his tallies, and wrote three short lines with his face close to the card, and blew on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:"He's not asked me anything in a long while. I can't speak for what he'll do with somebody new." He pushed the card along the counter with one finger. "No names. I don't want yours. If I've got it, I can be asked for it, and I've been asked for things before. You'll not have mine off my own tongue, either. It's on the card. That's as near as it comes to being said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:181:He passed within two strides of the yard, and of a boy of nineteen who stood against a doorpost with a bowl; and his red eyes did not come that way at all. They were on the floor, and they went along the flags, a joint at a time, as though he were reading how those had been laid as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:183:Cael had wished to be nobody in that yard, and he had been, and he ought to have been the easier for it. Some part of him had wanted the head to turn. He set it by, to be gone over later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:189:It was not the stop of someone who has left his change behind, which comes at the door, with a hand going to a pocket. This came a step late, as a cart stops after the horse has; and he stood with his back to the room, quite still, for as long as it takes to draw three breaths, while a porter with a frame went round him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:195:Cael knew what he had seen. He had watched Karis do the same thing at a bridge the morning before, so short that he had nearly walked a box into her. It was what a body did when its legs had gone on with the day, and the rest of it had only then taken in what had been set before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:201:She went up the ladder with the roll in one arm and the card in her teeth. She sat down on the nearest pallet, and took the card out of her mouth, and looked at it; and Cael saw that she had begun to shake, a little, and all over, as a person does who has come in out of the cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:"He did not ask what I thought. I have been waiting for somebody to ask me that for as long as I can remember, and the people who asked did not believe it, and I had grown used to that. I had it ready, all of it, in order, with the evidence under each head." She laid the card on her knee. "And he would not have it. He did not disbelieve me; he simply would not hear it yet, and I do not know what a person does with that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:He had been across the yard already. He had a bowl in his hand, from the hatch, with a spoon standing in it, and he set it on the boards between her boots. Then he stood there, as he had told a healer at a fork that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:221:Lira had the card, since she had lifted it off Karis's knee by its edges, as it had been held, and carried it to where the light came round the blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:223:"*Matter*," she read, with her lips. "*Its bearer, one. Hands—*" She stopped. "There's a figure. And his name, at the top, and a rank, and a number as long as my arm. And a day." She turned it over. The back was blank. "That's all that there is. That'd take five people through the buff gate and up to the top of everything. And it's not got one of them on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:225:He had read the card's head across her thumb, the wrong way up, as he read anything. It was five letters, in a blunt square hand, above a Path and a tier; he had heard that name once, and had thought it at a counter, and he had not before seen it written. She had not read it out, and he found that he did not care to either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:235:He did not push. He had seen her look at a blue coat from a smith's yard, the day before, with both her hands held still; and he thought that whatever she was working at had not finished with her yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:241:"The third gate," he said, and made a stroke. "From it, the street goes on, straight, and opens into the square within eighty yards, and that is the way which a person is told." He drew it, and then he laid the straw across it. "We do not take that. Under the wall, on the right hand as you come through, there is a lane, which loads use; and a keeper will point you to it if he is asked where loads go, and I should let him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:245:"There are two. Carts go to the left, by a street that runs the whole way about the crown, half a mile, to the yard's lower gate; and half way along it there is a bar, and a beam, where every load is weighed, and the weight is written against an owner." His eyes came up. "It is a book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:249:"The lane runs under the wall for two hundred paces, and there is no bar on it, since no cart could use what is at its end." He drew five short strokes, one below another, like the rungs of a ladder. "It is a cut, between two buildings, that goes down the back of the bluff to the same yard: a stair of five flights, which I made twenty-two steps. It is a stride and a half wide, and the walls on either hand are three men high and have no door in them, nor any window. Nobody stands in it. I went down it with a keg, and up again empty, and I did not meet a soul."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:257:"I shall take them as they come. I should prefer that to being weighed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:259:Nobody said who was to carry. There was a figure on that card, and three boxes had three backs under them; and there was a tarred case, and a man with a straw in his fingers who went ahead of every load and had never yet been counted with one. Cael marked it, as he marked anything that people took care to step round. It could be left until morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:273:He read the entry over, and it was thinner than what he had seen. Every reading that had ever been made of him had begun at the top, with a name, and a day, and a word, and had gone on down the sheet looking for whatever would bear the word out. At a cook-house counter he had watched a pair of sore eyes begin at the foot of a drawing, where the weight came down, and climb, and ask the paper for nothing that it had not offered. He did not know whether a person could be looked at so. He did not know whether he wished to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:275:The loft was quiet. Brom slept as he always slept, like something felled, with seven bowls stacked by his head, and Lira lay on her side with her back to the gable. Vastin stood at it, since he would not lie down while anybody else was awake, and the awl was still going in his fingers, in and out of a strap, by feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:277:Karis had not lain down either. She sat against the boxes, with the roll across her knees and both her hands flat on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:291:"As you did at the bridge. He stood for three breaths, and then he went up that hill like somebody who's left a pot on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:311:It was not a question. She had said it as she would have read a date off a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:325:She did not ask him what he meant. She was the one person he knew who never asked for the sentence after the one that she had been given. She only moved the roll, a little, so that the end of it lay across his knee as well as her own; and they sat so, under a slate roof, with fifty-two sheets between them, until the lantern guttered and Vastin at last put down the awl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:Seln had known it for some time. He had read two boards at two lane-ends, and both had said the same thing in the same paint: the gate was shut at sunset and opened at first light, and whoever was outside it slept outside it. A stick could not have done three miles in what was left of the day. Nobody said so, since nobody needed to; and it was Vastin himself who looked at the sun, and at the figure on the stone, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:9:There was a place made for it: a strip of trodden ground between the road and the river meadows, as long as four wagons, with a stone trough at one end and a ring of blackened stones at the other, and a thorn hedge along its weather side. A bank and a ditch ran the length of it at the road's edge, and carters who missed the gate had been pulling in there for longer than the post had stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:19:"You have been spoiled," said Karis. She was watching the flame as a person watches somebody else do her work badly. "So have I. I have not been cold at a fire-laying for years. I had not known that I minded."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:27:Seven bowls went round. Brom set the seventh on a flat stone a little apart, empty, and looked at it; and then he put a heel of bread in it, and took the bread out again, and ate it himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:31:Vastin had his bowl standing, in the gap at the hedge's end, across from Seln. He ate as he did everything, with attention, and he thanked Brom for it, once. His face was the grey of the stone that he leaned beside. He had come nine miles since noon on one leg and a stick; and when the bowl was empty he held it for a while, as though the setting of it down were a thing that would have to be planned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:Lira took it out of his hand as she went by with her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:39:The light went gold, and then it began to go. Cael felt Shadow stir under its seal, as it stirred at every dusk, and every other sound in him dropped as a yard goes quiet under snow. He told it no, and it lay down. He had been telling it so for longer than two years, and it had never once left off asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:53:It did not take a quarter of an hour, since he knew the road. At ten he had his own gait, slow now, as slow as he had ever felt it; and he went down the hiss of Wind without steering, and let it lean where it leaned. He did not go round a second time. He stopped a long way out, where the leaning began, and stood there, and asked the going-along the one thing that he had come to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:It felt the Wind. It felt the Wind lean. It felt the millstone, going by on the other hand, and the slow bellows drawing close in beside. It felt everything in him that went from one place to another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:Nothing came. It was not a silence, as he had been used to call it, since a silence is a thing that is heard. The tenth simply had no way of going where nothing went. He had the same nothing that he had had at noon, and that he had had three times before on other people's floors; and he knew it at last for what it was. He had laid a foot-rule against a smell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:65:So it would stand. He could write it. He sat a little longer against the stone, in the last of the light, and discovered that being right about it was no comfort at all. He had read himself five times now with the only thing he owned that read. He had used it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:73:"It's as good as another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:81:He turned his back to the bank. He set the stick's foot in the turf, a long way out, and took its head in both his raw hands. Then he let himself down by the strength of his arms, an inch at a time, with the bound leg held off the ground in front of him and the other bending under the whole of his weight. Cael saw the hands shake on the ash. He saw the left one, which did not open flat, slip a little, and take hold again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:83:He was down. He sat on the bank with one boot braced in the turf and the other leg stuck straight out over the ditch, and he was breathing as he had breathed at the top of the rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:"I read your file," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:"I read it for the first time in the week before I came up your bluff, on a road, by the candles of four inns, and for the last time on the morning when I left a seal in a tray. Between those two mornings I read it oftener than I have read anything that I did not write myself. I should like you to hear what was in it, since you have only ever seen it upside down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:107:Cael did not answer. He had sat across a counter at fourteen while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside a line, and he had learned his letters over again that year, the wrong way up, so that he should know what was being said of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:"When the folder had grown as thick as my hand," said Vastin, "the seat did what it does when its instruments have not served. It sent the one that it keeps for such cases. It sent me. I came up that road with a plate, and a set of questions, and three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:113:"Four feet. I measured it, as I measure everything." His voice had not altered at all, and it was that which made it hard to listen to. "I brought to those three days whatever forty years had given me: I have stood on eleven fields, and made something over nine thousand evaluations, and I can tell a man's rank from the way that he takes a stair, before he has reached the head of it. At the end of the third day I went down your hill with nothing whatever. The plate had lain as flat as a dish; every question had been answered, civilly, and I believe that every answer was true; and I could not have built one finding upon the whole of them. I had never in my life come away from a person without a figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:119:"I declined to read you a second time," said Vastin. "You know that. What you do not know is why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:121:"You told us. In a room at Ostrand, with five of us at a table." Cael kept his eyes on the moor. "You said there were three things you'd not do. That was the third. You said it was the only sentence you had left that they still needed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:127:"The order would have stood you on a plate again, in a room, at an hour, under the standard protocol. My certificate was wanted upon the protocol, and I had the request on my desk for longer than any paper has lain there. I did with it what I do with anything: I set out what a second reading could come to, and there were two things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:131:"It could be silent, as the first was; and then there would be two silences upon one file, and both would stand under my name. An office does not file two silences. I have sat in the rooms where that is settled, and a second blank is no longer the instrument's failure: it is entered as a quality of the subject; a word is found for it; and the word is set at the head of every sheet that follows. I had lately learned what that particular office did with words."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:135:"The other was that it would light." He said it very evenly. "That the plate would wake under your hand as it wakes under any child's, and give you a tier, and a Path, and a paper to carry. I should like to be able to tell you that I hoped for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:"I wrote *declined*, on a form which has no line for why; and I should have written it if there had been no such night. The reason that I gave you at Ostrand was the reason. I do not take it back." He shut his eyes. "But when the pen was down I found that I was glad, and it was not on your account that I was glad. I was relieved that I need never learn what the plate would say. I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one. I have been thought the better of since, by six people on a moor, for the half that I let them see. That is what I have carried from the seat to this bank, and it is not in the flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:163:"Then it is entered," said Vastin, "as what happened."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:165:The light was nearly gone. Cael looked along the bank at a brown coat and a pair of bound hands, and found that there was a thing in his own keeping which he was going to give this man. It surprised him. He had meant it for the book, and after the book for Lira, who read the book. But he had been paid tonight in a coin that he had not handled before, and he owned exactly one piece of the same mint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:171:"It was read again today. Twice. Once behind a hedge at a brook, and once here, against this stone, just before you came along."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:179:"I'll not tell you what read it, or how," said Cael. "That part's not only mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:183:"No. I saw you not ask." He drew a breath. "It was read with the one thing I've got that reads, and it came back with nothing. I don't mean that it's empty in there; I mean nothing came back. What I read with can feel a thing that moves, and whatever's in that place doesn't move, so the reading's got no way to say a word about it. I've been writing that nothing down for the better part of a year as if it were an answer. It wasn't one. It was only the thing I read with, telling me where it stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:185:Vastin had gone very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:191:"It is a sentence of mine. I have said it at inquiries for forty years, about frames." The left hand opened as far as it could, and shut. "I wrote it once of a person, in an assessment which a certain seat declined to receive, as lying outside its schedule; and I did not suppose that I should hear it said back to me on a roadside by the person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:197:"So you'd have had the first of your two," said Cael. "I can't swear it; a plate's not what I've got. But there's nothing in that place that moves, and I think a plate's the same sort of tool as mine. It would have lain flat, and I'd have been two blanks on one file, and they'd have found their word. Nobody has that but you. It's not in the book yet. I've not told her, and she's read every line I ever wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:203:"It is not more than it is," said Vastin. "I measured a person and could not read him, and tonight he has told me that he cannot read himself. I have had few friendships, and I know the look of them. This has not that look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:219:Then he took his hand away, and found his balance on the ash, and went back along the bank toward the fire. He went slowly, with the bound leg swinging; and he carried himself, Cael thought, like somebody who had set down the heavier of two things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:225:He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it, and the place: the three-mile post. Then he put down what he owed, before what he had found, as an old man in Denvash had taught him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:227:*Lira asked whether it was the thing I was told to leave alone. I said beside it. I read a healer's order for the leave I wanted in it. I'd do it again, and she's to be told so at the mill, by me, before anything else is said. Round, both times. Not in. Nothing taken up to it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:233:*Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:235:He stopped there, with the pencil's point on the leaf, and read the second line from the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:243:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Myself, twice: not counted, as before.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:247:Lira took it. She read as she always read, with her lips moving and her chin down, and she went through it twice. Then she laid her thumb on the leaf, beside one line, and did not say the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:253:"No. I know that." She shut the book over her thumb. "I never asked for it. I want that in there as well. I could have, and I'd have got it, and you'd have hated me for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:273:Across the fire Brom was showing Karis how a flint was held. She was attending as she attended to a stratum, and striking her own knuckle. Seln stood at the hedge's end. Vastin was on his feet in the gap, with both hands on his stick, looking at nothing; but he was looking at it, Cael thought, as a man looks who has for once no order that he is in the middle of drafting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:275:The town lay behind them. He could tell that it was there by the two carters, who sat on the other side of their own fire and faced it, as people face a hearth. There would be lights along the lowest wall by now, and in the gate towers. Higher up there would be one that was pale, and did not flicker.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:9:"There's no draught," said Brom. "It's as tight as a cask in here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:13:Nobody argued with her. Brom looked at the blanket for a moment, and at Cael's back, and began to lay the three boxes in a row under the eaves with their beckets to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:15:Karis had not sat down. She stood in the middle of the loft with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as though she had been set there by somebody who would be back for her directly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:19:"Because of the blue coats," said Brom. "I'm Copper. That's this street and the one behind it. I read the boards."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:21:"Iron walks through," said Lira. She was shaking out a pallet, and she spoke as she would have spoken of the weather. "That's what the blue is. It's not a pass at all, up there; it's what the place is for. You show the paper and you go by."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:31:"The man below counts, since feet are all that he is asked for; the men above do not count those who come by right. They read. A certificate is a name, and the district that issued it, and a number, and it is entered at the gate, with the hour, and goes up to a counter before night to be laid against a list." He did not raise his voice. "There are four names in this loft which must not be written in any book inside that wall. I told you why on the ninth day, and a man who used to certify such papers told you again at a board. One hour is what the paper allows; you would not be at the third gate before it was served."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:37:So that left one; and Cael felt the loft come round to it, as he had once felt a table take care not to look at himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:45:It was said without heat. He looked at each of the five in turn, as he looked at a room, and gave every one of them the same length of time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:47:"They would open both of those gates, and a good deal more than the gates: a man in a pale coat would come down that hill to meet me, at a run, with a clerk behind him carrying a chair." His mouth moved very slightly. "And from that minute you would not be six people with boxes. You would be an officer's party; your number would be taken, and your business, as a courtesy to me, and the whole of it would be minuted by evening and read by somebody whom I cannot name to you. I do not know who reads this town's books at night. A cold term ago I should have said that I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:He went down the ladder without a sound. Karis looked after him; and then she lowered herself onto the nearest pallet, very carefully, with a courier's flat held in both arms against her chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:65:"Which way?" said Lira. She had sat up as soon as his boots moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:69:She saw it at once. With the wall on his left, the hill would be on his left as well; and whatever stood on the crown of the hill would be behind his shoulder for the whole of the walk, where he need do nothing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:73:A road ran round the foot of the bluff, under the second wall, and he found it by going uphill until he could go no farther. It was cobbled, and it was narrow, and the houses on its upper side had been built straight against the wall's foot, so that their back rooms were the town's own masonry. He walked with one hand almost on their doors. The wall went up above the roofs on his left, pale and sheer, as high as the scree in a cut that he remembered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:83:He watched for a quarter of an hour, by the shadow of the butt, and it was as plain as a mill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:87:There were strangers who came by right. He saw only one, a young man in travelling boots, who had the look of a courier. He took a folded paper from his coat without being asked, and the keeper with empty hands opened it and read it all, and read out a name and a district and a rank to his fellow; and the pen wrote for as long as it takes to say a line. The young man had his paper back and went up. He had been perfectly easy. He had been written in a book as a person is who has never had cause to mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:91:They came up the street in knots, with frames on their backs, and each knot had one card among it with a blue edge. The keeper took the card. He did not look at a single face. He looked at the figure on the card, and then he pointed with the feather of his pen at each cap as it went under the arch, and his lips moved. When the caps and the figure agreed, he made one stroke on his sheet, tore the corner off the card, and gave the card back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:95:A girl came up with a basket of eggs. She had a card. The keeper read it, and shook his head, and tapped the card with his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:101:"I know they're fresh. They'll be fresh in the morning." He was not unkind about it. He gave her the card, and pointed down the hill with the pen; and she went and sat on the kerb with the basket on her knees, and looked at the arch as a person looks at a shop that has shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:109:He had no tier of any sort. He stood by a water-butt, four strides from two men who read people for their living, with a box-strap's mark still red on his neck; and neither of them had so much as wondered what he was. There was no sieve in that town fine enough to hold him. He had fallen clean through the lowest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:115:It came out above the river on the bluff's far side, where the town wall ran along a quay, and there was a landing. He had not seen a flat boat since Lowmarch. There were six of them here, tied nose to tail, and a great wooden wheel on the quay with a man walking inside it, round and round, so that a sack went up on a rope at every dozen steps. A ferry lay at the landing's lower end, on a cable. He looked at the cable's blocks for Brom's sake, and could not have said what lay the rope was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:A leaf was shown, with great ceremony.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:171:He had written two sentences in a book, at a fire, for a healer who would not take his word. They had been easy to write. The square had been two days off, in a town that he had never seen, and a thing which is two days off costs nothing to forgo. He had not known that it would stand at the head of the only street; that the record he had walked here for would be kept behind a door on its far side; or that the two of them would face one another across forty yards of paving, like a pair of people who have been set to keep each other honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:191:He had known it, of course. An officer of his grade did not arrive; he was announced, by a form, four days before, and the form went to the very people whom he came to look at. So he had ridden for forty years through streets that had been swept the day before, past whitewashed kerbs, to offices where every book lay squared on its desk and a man stood at the sill with his cap in his hand. He had allowed for it, as a careful officer allows for the known error of a scale, and had supposed that he could subtract the whitewash and arrive at the street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:203:He was off his watch, by the unbuttoned collar; but he had kept the blue coat on, and he sat in the one chair that had a back. A bowl had come to him through the hatch before he asked for it. He had not paid. The carters along the bench had moved up for him as water moves for a boat, without looking round, and the bench had closed again behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:215:There was a paper under his hand, where it had stopped, and it was not the only one. He carried, in oilskin, three sheets under one seal, and they were what he had told a loft that he would not show; and beside them lay a receipt in a small upright hand, unsealed, which said that certain things had been read in part and understood in no part. He had read that sentence a good many times on the road, and he thought it the more exact document of the two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:219:A boy had given it to him on a bank, at dusk, across a yard of turf. He had it by heart, as he had always had a finding: there was a place, which a healer had found; it had been read twice in a day; nothing had come back. He did not know what had read it. A man of his training could set out the possibilities, in their order, and on the road that morning he had begun to; and he had reached the second, and seen that it was a draft, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:223:Out under the arch, a clerk in an apron had come to the bench across the way. Four people got up. A card went into a cap, and the four went off up the hill with frames on their backs, and not one of them had been asked so much as a name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:225:He watched them out of sight. The lowest thing in that district went through its walls on the least ink. He set that beside the keeper's bowl, as a fact about a mechanism, and could not have said why he had troubled to keep it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:It was the large young man who had set it there, and he had six others on a board. Vastin felt for a copper. He was told, as he had been told on four evenings running, to put it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:231:"I had not known," he said, "that a man could be fed for four days and still be owed nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:233:"You've not been fed. You've been counted." Brom set the seventh bowl at the table's end, by itself. "It's different. Eat it while it's hot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:241:"I did not go above the trough," said Seln. He sat down on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung, which was as near as he came to resting. "I hired a frame for a copper, and I sat for three hours on the porters' bench at the top of the street, and was twice offered work. The man beside me has carried to the registry's yard for eleven years, and he likes to be listened to. I have four things from him, and one difficulty. I shall give them in his order."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:249:"He carries to a yard; he has not been below the door, and he knows nobody who has." He inclined his head to her, by a very little. "There is one door, in the undercroft beneath the yard, and it is of iron. He says that the registry's wall is built round its frame, and he meant it as a complaint against the masons."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:275:The small pale woman on the pallet had turned her head. She was looking at the row under the eaves, where three boxes lay with their beckets to the wall and a tarred case leaned against the last; and her lips moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:283:She had turned her own over in the lantern-light, the right and the slow left, as though she had been asked to produce them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:297:"He's spent his whole life being a name with a word after it," she said, across the lantern. "And the one place that might know what the word means will have him as a stroke of a pen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:321:"It is the only way to the front door, and a load does not go in at a front door. There is a yard, and a yard has a gate, and carts must reach it by some road which is not a paved square with a station's steps on it." Seln got up off the ladder's head. "I have not found that road. I shall find it tomorrow; and tomorrow at noon somebody will stand at a cook-house counter with a sheet in her hand that a certain man cannot look away from. That is as far as I have got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:323:Karis laid her pencil down on the flat. Her hand was not quite steady.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:333:"A name and a reason," Karis said. "Older than the walls, under our feet, and nobody's read it because nobody's had a reason to look and a person who could read it in the same lifetime." Her hand lay flat on the courier's leather, and it had stopped shaking. "That's us. That's the whole reason it's still buried. We're the first ones who are both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:It was done as a thing is passed over a counter. The man in the dusty coat had shot the bar home behind them, a slide of black iron as long as his arm, with the flat of his palm; and he turned from it, and found a free hand at the height of his own, and hung the lamp's ring upon its fingers. He did not look to see whose hand it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:Cael stood on the first tread and listened to him go. The steps were quick and perfectly even, with never a hand put out to the wall, and they grew small as a dropped stone grows small in a well. A man who has gone up and down one stair for the better part of his life does not need to see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:They went down in the order that the stair allowed. Lira went first, with the lamp held low at her knee, as she had seen it held; and the light went before her boots in a small yellow round, three treads at a time, and showed nothing beyond them. Karis followed with her bare hand on the wall. Brom came after her, bent under his box, since the roof of that stair had been cut for shorter people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:19:The stick came last but one. Cael went below it, backward, as he had gone down a slot in the open air an hour ago, with his heels feeling for each tread and his palms on the stone at either side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:27:The treads were of the dark stone, and so were the walls; each block lay against its neighbour without mortar, so close that his fingers could not find the joint until they had passed it. A sill above had been rubbed bright by more feet than he could reckon; here, a dozen steps beneath it, the edges were as sharp as on the day they were cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:31:The cold deepened with every flight. He had felt cold in a good many places since the Line: on a mountain, and on a river that had turned into a road, and in a shed with the hurdles tied up against the wind. All of those had been moving. This one stood. It lay on the stair like water in a cistern, and when he went down into it he felt it close over his knees, and his belt, and his collar, a tread at a time. The flame in the lamp did not lean. It stood up as straight as a painted one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:He marked that. Anchor was the eighth of the ten things that he carried, a piece of the craft by which the Compact holds a person where he stands; it had asked his leave to bar every sill and gate since Ostrand, and at an iron door it had asked very low. It asked nothing here. There was nothing in that place to set a bar across, and the fragment seemed to know so before he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:37:"Twenty-seven," said Vastin, on the floor. He was breathing through his nose, and he stood for a moment with both hands on the ash and the bound leg thrust out. "He can count as well. I had begun to think it a rare accomplishment in this town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:They were in a passage that ran across the stair's foot, a dozen strides from end to end. The dark stone lined it. Where the lining stopped, on the far side, there were three openings, side by side, each as broad as a cart and half as high again as Brom; and each was a square of perfect black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:41:Karis had gone straight to the nearest. She laid her palm on the place where the dark blocks ended, and then on what came after them, and Cael saw her go quite still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:"Later," said Brom. He said it as he had said it in the yard, gently, and he was looking at the three mouths. "Which one did he go down?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:47:Nobody could have told by listening, for there was nothing to hear. It was Lira who found it. She had lifted the lamp, and lowered it again at once, as though the man could have seen her from wherever he was; and then she put it behind her back altogether, so that her own body shut the light off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:The shelves began a stride inside the mouth and ran on down both walls, in bays, each bay as wide as a man's two arms and divided by an upright of oak that the years had turned black. The lamp showed four strides of them at a time. Cael saw bundles tied in tape, laid flat, and rolls stood on end in racks like spears; he saw boxes of thin wood with no mark on them, and things in sacking, and a whole bay of boards, each as long as a forearm, stacked like slates. Nothing had a ticket. He looked for one in bay after bay, as he would have looked on any shelf in any office that he had ever stood in, and did not find so much as a number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:69:The floor was the bluff's own rock, cut level, and it was clean. No dust lay on it as dust lies in a loft; there was only a fine pale bloom along the foot of each bay, where nobody walked, and it was the colour of a certain coat. The air smelled of dry paper, and of old glue, and of cold stone, and under those of nothing whatever. It was the smell of a book that has stood shut on a shelf since before its reader was born.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:75:He had spoken very low, as people do in such places. The stick had slowed. He was looking at a bay of rolls as he had looked at a bridge with five piers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:83:At ninety strides the shelves on the right hand gave place to tables. They stood end to end down the middle of the gallery, long and dark and as heavy as church doors, and Cael knew what they were from a hall above Ostrand: somebody had once sorted paper here by the cartload. They were heaped. Bundles lay on them a foot deep, squared, with slips of card thrust between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:87:He saw that before he saw the man, and it stopped his counting at a hundred and four. Three tables had been cleared to the wood, and wiped; and along the floor beside each, in rows as straight as a clerk's ruling, stood what had been taken off them. Each bundle lay under a slip, and the slips ran in an order. Somebody had lifted the work of years from where it lay, a piece at a time, and set it down on cold rock so that it might be put back exactly; and he had done it between yesterday's noon and this morning's second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:91:Beyond the bare tables the gallery ended, in a wall of the pale rock with the pick's strokes still on it. A high desk stood against that wall, sloped, of the same black oak as the shelves; and over the desk, on an iron arm let into the stone, a lamp burned inside a chimney of glass. It was a far better light than the one that Lira carried. It lay on the desk's slope like a cloth, and on a leaf that lay there, and on the bent grey head of the man who was reading it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:97:He did not turn round. He raised his left hand a little from the desk, with the palm toward them, as a carter checks a horse; and then he laid it down again, and went on reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:99:"Tables," said Cleon, to the leaf in front of him. "There are three bare. Which place is which?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:"Then eleven on the nearest. Twenty-nine on the long one; it'll take them in three rows. Twelve on the last." His finger had not left its line, and he had not so much as lifted his head. "As they lay on the floors. North to the stair. If two sheets met on the stone, they meet on my table, and if there was a gap, I want the gap."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:Brom looked at Cael, and Cael lifted one shoulder, under the strap. They got the loads off. Brom let his own box down to the floor by inches, with his knees, so that it should make no noise in that place; and the third came off Cael's back, with a third part of fifty-two sheets in it and a purse sealed under five thumbs, for the first time since a frost in an inn yard. Karis did not put anything down. She stood with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, at the very rim of that good light, and her face had gone as still as water in a butt. She had waited the whole of her life for this room. It appeared that she could wait while a stranger came to the end of his paragraph.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:115:He did not think that anybody had kept that man standing at the bottom of a room in forty years. Books had been held out to him, open, at the gate of this very town. He stood now with a case on his shoulder, behind a girl with a lamp, while a Bronze of the least-regarded class on the roll gave his orders over one shoulder and went on with his page; and Cael saw the right hand leave the stick, and hang, and go back. The old mouth had set. Then, slowly, as a thing is done that has been practised, the grey eyes came away from the reader's back and went to the shelves, and stayed there, on a bay of boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:119:The man stood at the head of the first bare table, with his hands behind him. The leaf lay on the slope as it had lain, face upward under the good light, with nothing put across it to keep the place; he had come to the foot of it and walked away, and not one of five people had seen him do either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:121:"That comes with me." His red eyes had gone to the lamp, and to nothing that was near it. "Low. As low as you carried it in. A hand to the left of whatever I'm at; not on it, and not behind me, and not where I've been. Is that understood?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:131:She had the second box unroped on the floor, and the oilcloth open, and she carried the sheets to their tables one at a time, flat on her two forearms, as a person carries a dish that is full to the brim. She set each down by a measure that she had in her notebook. Twice she took one up again and moved it the width of a finger. The man in the dusty coat stood at the end of the first table through all of it, with his eyes shut and his hands behind him, and waited as she had waited for his page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:135:He did not read. Cael had seen that at a cook-house counter, and he saw it now for two hours together, from four strides off, with nothing to do but watch. The man bent over the first of the Ring's eleven, at its southern edge, where the foot of the sheet lay nearest him; and his eyes went up it as a mason's go up a wall that he has been asked to buy, stopping wherever a thing seemed to bear weight, and going back down from it to see what it stood on. When they came to the top they went to the next sheet's foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:147:She had. The left had come up to steady the ring, slowly, as it came to everything; and the flame had not so much as shivered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:151:So Brom knelt with it. He got down as he did everything, without haste, and held the ring in his left fist as he would have held a plumb-line; and for the length of three sheets nothing was said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:165:The long table took the man the greater part of the time. He went down its three rows from the south, and came back, and went down them again; and there was a place in the second row where he stood so long that Karis took a step toward him, and checked herself. He said nothing. He went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:171:Karis heard that. She did not turn her head; but her gloved hand shut on the edge of the flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:175:He stopped over that one as a dog stops at a gate. He looked at it from its foot. Then he went round the table's corner, and looked at it from the side, with his head laid over; and he came back, and bent until his face was a hand's breadth from her pencil, and stayed so for longer than he had given to any six of the others. Lira brought the light round to his left side, a hand off, without being told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:183:Cael had heard her say that word once before, at a trestle under a marsh, and shut her mouth upon it afterward as upon a box. It was a registrar's word, which the Compact had once written in square brackets against whatever its plates could not class, and had long since struck out of its books; and it was the oldest thing that she knew how to date by. She had said then that she would not say it twice. She had kept it for this table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:The man knew it. Cael saw that he did, though his eyes did not open: the square hands tightened upon the wood, and were still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:187:He asked nothing more. He let go of the table, and walked to his desk with one hand before him, by habit, as a man crosses his own bedroom in the dark. There was a shelf above the desk, under the lamp's iron arm, and on it a cup, and a jar, and a thick bundle tied with tape; and at its end a long flat thing wrapped in sacking. He took that down. He stood with his back to all of them, and turned the sacking open, and looked at whatever lay in it. He looked from its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:197:He felt in his coat, and brought out a square of card with a buff edge, and went back to the slope of his desk to write it. It took him three lines, with his face close to the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:199:"Tomorrow. Second bell, as before." He pushed the card along the desk with one finger and did not look to see who took it. "Your boxes go up when the bell goes, and they come down with you in the morning; I'll not keep what I'm not reading. What's on those tables stays on those tables. Nobody squares it. Nobody leans on it. Nobody breathes over it with his mouth open."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:203:"Today I'm no use to you. I'll be an hour getting these back." He had sat down in the chair that stood at his desk, heavily, with his hands flat on his knees and his eyes shut. "Put the lamp on the table's end, where I can find it. Then do what you like, so long as it's quiet, and it's nowhere near my paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:209:She stood at its corner, where a square pale hand had rested, and looked down at the fourth of the Hall's twelve. Cael came and stood beside her. He had left the box against a bay. The carried lamp burned at the table's end, a yard from them, where Lira had set it down as carefully as an egg; and the other, on its iron arm, lit a grey head that did not move. The desk was not six strides off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:He looked at the sheet, since she was looking at it, and could not have said why a man should stop at that one. He was reading it the wrong way up, from the far side of the table, as he read everything; and it came to him that he stood at the sheet's foot, where the man himself had begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:223:She was shaking again. It was not as she had shaken in a hayloft, with a card on her knee. He saw her take hold of it, a breath at a time; and she did not answer him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:231:Karis had turned round. She was looking at the grey head as a person looks who has heard her own trouble told in a stranger's voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:245:"I do not know. He has seen how the thing stands, for two hours, and he has not yet said what he saw." Karis took up the bread, and looked at it, and set it down. "It is his to say how long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:251:The eyes shut again. It had not been put as a thing that anybody was likely to refuse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:257:"I shall take them as they come," said Vastin. "I believe that I have said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:261:Karis had turned back to them. She was the smallest person in that gallery, and she stood with her back to fifty-two sheets, as she had once stood at a broker's plank with a hide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:281:Nobody had forbidden it. There was a term against a carried light, and he carried none; and a man with shut eyes had said that they might do as they liked, so long as it was quiet. He walked on the rock between the black uprights, counting, and the two flames drew together behind him into one. At forty strides the bundles on either hand had lost their edges. At sixty he could tell the shelves only as a person tells the walls of his own room at night, and he stopped there, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:285:He had been in a great many buildings since he was fourteen, and in every one of them there had been a drawer. There was a counter at Denvash, with a pen on it. There was a house on a bluff, where a file had grown as thick as a man's hand; there was a district seat, and a borrowed office. He had learned to feel for the drawer on any threshold, as a dog feels for the one board in a floor that gives. Even on the kind side of the Line there had been a book, kept by a man with one arm; that one had been honest, and he had been glad to stand in it, and it had put him eleventh all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:289:Paper rose on both sides of him to the roof, more of it than a hall above Ostrand had held, and not a leaf of the whole had a line for what he was. It could not have had. Whoever had tied those tapes had done it before there was a plate to stand a child on, or a word in square brackets to put after his name; he had that on the word of a small pale woman, who did not give her word lightly, and of a man who had gone without his dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:291:It was a strange thing to find in himself. He would have expected to feel light; and he did, a little, as he had felt at a hedge when a rule was lifted off a road. But he did not know how to stand. He had braced against that drawer for so long that his shoulders had forgotten any other way to hang, and he stood between two walls of other people's records with his hands open and nothing to set them against.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:299:It was a sound that brought him round. It was small and flat, like a spoon struck once on a cup, and it came from the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:303:They roped the boxes and went up. The man came behind them as far as the iron, and drew the bar, and stood back against the wall with his eyes on their boots. The clerk was waiting in the vault with his lantern, exactly where he had been left, as though he had not moved since the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:305:The cut was harder going up. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, and Cael went two steps below him, where a man would fall. On the bottom flight a tread rocked under Brom's weight, and he stopped, and trod on it again to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:309:It was dusk in the lane under the wall. The four alleys had lost their white. There was a paleness at the far end of each now which did not come from any sky, and it lay along the cobbles in a bar that did not flicker. Cael walked through all four with an old man's breath labouring at his shoulder; and a box went by on his left hand each time, between him and it, at the height of a girl's head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:311:Seln was at the ladder's foot when they came in under the arch. He looked the five of them over as a man counts his change.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:313:"Three people came to look at that low door in the yard, when it had shut on you," he said. "Two were yard-men, and they looked as people look at a thing which they have never seen used. The third was the clerk. He came out upon his own sill, with his book under his arm, and stood there for as long as it takes to read a page. Then he went in again to his hatch and sat down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:11:"Then lie still for a corner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:The old man stood at the gable, under the blanket, as he stood every night until the last of them was down. He had an awl in his fingers again. There was nothing left on the tarred case that wanted stitching, and he had begun on the becket of Cael's box, without asking, where the rope had started to fray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:Shadow had lifted at dusk, in the lane, between the second alley and the third. Cael had told it no while a bar of pale light went over his boots. It lay shut now, and the other nine had come back, each to its own place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:19:When the lantern went out he lay with his hands behind his head and listened to the loft, as he had listened to every room that he had ever slept in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:21:A room full of sleepers has a shape, and he had known this one's for five nights. Brom made the most of it, in the middle, breathing like something felled. Lira lay on her right side, a yard from Cael's elbow, and Karis by the boxes, small and quick, with a catch at the top of every breath that had been there since a cook-house. There was Vastin's, upright, by the gable. And there was a place near the ladder's head where nothing was. He had learned to know it as a person knows where a rug lies in a dark room, because the floor does not ring there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:25:He heard nothing. The ladder did not creak; it never had, under that one. There was only a difference in the dark by the ladder's head, as when a coat that has hung on a door for a week is taken down, and the door is seen to be a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:43:It was harder than holding a lamp. He stopped wanting to be at the ladder, as he had stopped wanting to be anywhere else on a floor of cold rock, and after a time it held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:53:It was a quiet district at night. He had expected that. A town which lets its labour in by the piece does not keep it up after dark; the benches were empty, and the slates had been wiped, and the only wheels that he heard were the watch's handcart, two streets off, going its round with a lantern. He let it go by. Then he went up to the ring road, and along under the wall, as far as the chandler's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:57:He went up it, and out along the leads, and sat down with his back against a chimney that was still warm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:He had not seen it so before. He had gone up it once as a counted hand, with his eyes where a porter's eyes are, on the setts; and he had watched a yard from a bench, and a door from a parapet. From here it was laid out as a plan is. The second wall went round below his feet, with the blue gate in it, shut, and a brazier burning in the arch where two keepers stood their night. Above that the slate roofs climbed, close and dark. Above the slates ran the third wall, the old one, low and pale; and over the wall, like the back of a great book stood on a shelf, the registry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:He looked at that for some time. It was small, at such a distance, and pale, and it did not flicker. Every station that he had ever served under had burned so; a man who works in a registry leaves off seeing it within a month, as he leaves off hearing the clock. He had not been inside a wall with one for longer than he cared to reckon. He saw it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:73:It weighed something under three pounds. He had known that weight for twelve years, to the ounce, as a man knows the weight of his own arm; which is to say that he had not felt it since the first month. It had gone through three postings on him, and across a Line. It had stood for some months between a researcher's grey boxes, on a shelf, with its key in his waistcoat, and had gone back onto the strap on the night that five people left a hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:75:He felt it tonight. It pulled a little on the left side, as a debt does.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:He had opened the one: he had set it between two hands on a rock, and gone to stand by the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:85:This was a night, and he had nothing in his sleeve. He looked down at the dark leather against his coat, squarely, as he would have looked at another man's file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:89:There were three doors to that building, as there are to every registry on the Line, and he had used the third for twelve years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:He had passed it three days ago, under a keg. He had not needed to turn his head. He knew how it would be: a slot of light, and a night clerk behind it, half asleep, with a book. The courier lays his packet on the ledge. The clerk enters it by its seal and its weight, and tears off a slip. Neither is permitted to ask the other a question. It is the oldest rule in the service, and the only one that he had ever wholly respected. A registry enters what is laid on its ledge. It may bury the thing afterward, or burn the copy, or hold it for six years in a drawer where nobody will receive it; but for the length of one line, in one book, on one night, the thing has been set down as having come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:A line would be written. *One case, locked. By hand.* It would go up in the morning to a counter. Somebody would open it there, with a key or without one. He could not see past that. He had been able to see past it once; he had known the names of the men who read that sort of return, and what each of them wanted, and which would carry it up a stair and which would carry it down. An old officer had stood in a hayloft four days ago and said that he no longer knew who read this town's books at night. If that man did not know, a copying clerk could hardly pretend to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:There were four names, besides, which must not be written inside a wall. He had counted an hour for them, on a ladder. What was in the case had those names nowhere in it, and it led to them all the same, as a road leads to a house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:103:So it was not that door, and he had known it before he sat down. He had come up here to learn whether he still wished that it were, and he found that he could not tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:115:He found, when he had been still for a while, that one matter had changed, and it was not the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:It did not rule on anything. He set it beside the case, as a fact about doors which he had not possessed before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:He stood as Seln would have stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:131:He had ruled nothing. He came down the hoist-stair as he had gone up, and the man in the doorway was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:137:He had not heard the ladder. He straightened, with the water running off his chin, and there was a small grey man beside the trough, with his hands in his sleeves, looking as he looked on every morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:153:He said nothing more. Cael waited, with his hair dripping, since it was plain that something was meant to follow; and nothing did. The man turned, and went to see whether the half-door was open, as though he had given a full report and been dismissed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:163:He counted the second of those, as he counted anything. It was six words. He did not think that he had ever written a larger entry about that man, or one of which he understood less.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:169:Karis looked at the cushion for as long as it takes to draw a breath. She did not sit. She had the plank before she had her gloves off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:173:"The clusters at the row's end. The pecks, and the rings. Are they of the same cutting as the rows?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:"Same tool. Same day, near enough." He did not turn round. "They're not packing. They bear nothing, and nothing's hung on them; but they're not red. They stand outside the row, like a figure at the foot of a bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:183:Cael watched her from the table's end, where the lamp was not yet wanted. She counted the signs in the first row with the point of her pencil in the air: twelve. Then she looked at what stood after it. There were two rings and two pecks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:185:She did the second row. Nine signs; a ring, and four pecks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:191:"The third is twelve again. And the last is seven: a ring, and two." Her voice had gone thin and quick. "Those are figures. I can read his figures. I have not been able to read one mark of this in a year, and I can read how many."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:203:Cael took the ring from Lira at the Stair's table, as he had on the two days before. He saw a raw hand come out, a little ahead of his own. It had not come far. It stopped a foot from the ring and hung there, and went back to the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:209:The man with the reed went through the Stair's twenty-nine that morning, and it was slow. Karis laid, and wrote, and laid. Somewhere in the second hour Cael's forearm began to tell him how long it had been lying on his thigh. He shifted his fingers on the ring, by as little as he could; and the shadow of a white pebble moved across her pencil by the width of a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:213:He had it still again before the word was out. But he had heard the stick. It had come down once, hard, a stride nearer; and when he looked from under his brows the old man had planted it across his own path, like a bar across a gate, and stood behind it with his jaw set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:221:At noon the man went up for his bowl, and the lamp stood on the table's end, as it was ordered. The flame had begun to smoke a little at one corner. Any of them could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:223:Vastin crossed to it. He went as he would have gone to a clerk who had blotted a line, with his right hand coming up already to turn the wick; and he was standing over the thing, with two fingers a hand's breadth from the brass, before he remembered what had been said about touching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:Cael did not tell him. He sat against a table's leg, with a heel of bread, and watched a man who had held an office in every room that he had entered for forty years stand in one that had exactly a single office in it. It was a lamp. It was held by a boy of no tier, on one knee, at a stranger's word, and it was the only thing in the whole of that gallery that anybody was doing for anybody else. The old man could not kneel. He had one hand. He was drawn to it all the same, as a nail is drawn across a table to a lodestone, and each time he found himself half way there without knowing that he had set out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:237:The man had gone back to the plank, to look at the head of it, where the first row began; and for that he wanted the light high.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:241:It was a tall man's task. Cael knew it as he rose. He was the shortest man in the gallery but one, and he would have to hold the ring at the full stretch of his arm; Brom had one arm in a knot and the other hanging; and there was a person by the nearest bay who stood half a head above any of them, and held himself like a post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:It took one step, and stopped. Cael saw Vastin look at his own boot, which had gone forward, as he might have looked at a clerk who had spoken out of turn. Nobody had asked him. The word had been *lamp*, and he was not the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:249:Cael stood, and put his arm up, and held the flame above the plank's head for as long as it was wanted. It was a good while. His shoulder was shaking before the end, and he let it shake, and kept the wrist still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:257:"I asked you for one, the first day, and I've spent the half of it on my own page; I've read that every morning before I'd so much as look at yours. That's put by. A week. Mine. From today, and nothing else on the desk. And at the end of it the whole lot goes up that stair, every sheet, and the fellow with the green book writes that it's gone." He rubbed his eyes. "He entered three boxes three days back, and he's written *the same* under them ever since. If they lie on my tables a month, he'll begin to wonder what *the same* is. I've been asked for things before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:265:"That came off the same shelf as the board. It was behind it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:273:"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." His chin went toward the three tables, and his eyes did not leave her. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." The two fingers found the brown tape again, behind him, without his looking for it. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." He let the hand fall from the shelf. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:289:"Four times," said Vastin, when he could. "I counted them, as the young woman did. I had no business with that lamp. I have one hand to give it, and a leg that will not kneel, and nobody in that place had asked me for either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:293:"I went toward it as a man goes toward his own name when it is called in a street."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:307:It was not asked as a question. Cael heard what it was. An officer was asking the person he had once sat across a table from, with a plate between them, how a thing was learned which that person had been made to learn by officers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:The keepers in blue had seen it now. One of them said *lower archive* to the other, as a man says the name of a bird that he saw once and has now seen twice; then the feather of his pen went along five caps without stopping at any. The old keeper at the round arch read the seal from its stamp inward, exactly as before, and said *one, and four*, and told them again to mind their lame one on the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:9:In the cut the third tread of the bottom flight rocked under Brom, as it had rocked the evening before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:"Still wants bedding," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:The yard was as it had been: a wagon at the dock, and a man with a slate at the beam, and nobody who looked round. There was a porter on the bench under the hatch, at its far end, a small man with a carrying-frame between his knees and his cap pulled down, asleep. Cael gave him the glance that he gave to a post. He had been told, on a ladder's head, not to look twice at anything in that yard which was not a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:21:He counted the boxes with his eyes, as he had the day before, and he dipped his pen. Then he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:It was a small thing to see. The pen had gone down to the leaf, and it did not write. It hung above a clean line, and the clerk looked at the three boxes on the paving, and at the tarred case, and then he laid his left hand on the book and turned it back by one leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:Cael had not heard him speak before; it was a thin voice, dry and exact, with a short stop after each thing, as though the words were being taken one at a time out of a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:"Three boxes, corded. One surveyor's case, tarred. One courier's flat. One hide, rolled. Brought down for study on the two hundred and eighty-fourth." He read it from his own line, with his finger under it. Then he looked up, at the small pale woman on the other side of his ledge; and it was the first thing that he had ever asked any of them. "The same?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:49:He saw the whole of it, as he would have seen a bad landing a stride before his foot came down. An officer of the registry would come down four flights with a clerk behind him. He would not count. He would look at each of them in turn, as an officer must, and he would have a pen; and the first thing that any such man had ever said to Cael, on any floor, was *and you are?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:59:Vastin knew the ruling. Cael understood that in the same breath, and too late. There was not a schedule in the code that the man could not have said from memory, with its article, and for forty years he had been the person sent for when a clerk could not tell how a thing was to be put. A clerk was in difficulty over a form, six feet away from him. He had gone to it as a healer goes to a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:65:The clerk's hand had gone to his pen. It was not done on purpose, any more than the other thing had been; a man who has sat at a hatch all his life hears a name and a rank and an office, in their proper order, and his fingers go for the ink. The pen was up, and it was coming over the leaf toward the margin, where a registry writes whatever it is told at its own door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:73:"He's on the count. Four hands, same as yesterday, entered by the practitioner and answered for by her, and the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was. The researcher's at your counter and she'll want to be told we're through," Seln said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:79:The clerk looked at the small grey man who had spoken. He looked at him properly, from the cap to the boots, as he had not looked at any of the four; and Cael watched a face go into a memory that kept things. Then the clerk looked at the old one, who stood with his mouth still a little open upon a word that he had not been let to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:He lifted the pen away from the leaf, and carried it, very steadily, clear of the book's edge, and touched its point to the cuff of his right sleeve, where there was ink already. The bead went into the grey cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:83:"A hand," he said, to nobody. He turned his eyes down to his own line of the day before, and his lips moved over it. "Brought down. For study." He looked at the three boxes once more. Something eased in his face then, slowly, as a knot eases when the right end is found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:85:He dipped, and wrote two words under yesterday's entry, and the day after them. Cael read them the wrong way up, across the ledge. *The same.* The pen went to the narrow column, and made its single mark; and it went on to the narrower one at the edge of the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:For fifteen years no desk had been able to say what Seln looked like, and that was the whole of his trade. A clerk remembered furniture, and he had taken care to be furniture that was never spoken to; he had kept off a card yesterday for exactly that. Today he had stood up in a yard, and shown his face to a man who forgot nothing, and joined it with his own mouth to four porters and a card. He would not sit on that bench again, and Cael did not think that he would so much as walk through that yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The door had opened. The clerk stood on its sill, with the book shut under his arm, and he looked at Vastin once, as a man looks at an inkwell that has gone over and has by some mercy missed the page. Then he stood aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:109:He put his cap on. He swung the frame to his shoulder, as a porter does who has given up waiting for a load, and walked away down the yard toward the carts' gate, without haste, among the wagons; and he did not look back at a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:115:Cael went below the stick, as he had the day before. The man in the dusty coat had counted three boxes and a case at the iron, with his eyes on the floor, and gone ahead of them into the dark; he had seen nothing in an old porter's face, since he did not look at faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:125:"The name, the rank, and the office, in the order of the roll. I did not decide to do it. I heard it as a man hears a clock strike in his own house; and by the time that I knew what the sound was, it had struck three." The stick's foot grated on the stone. "He had his pen up. He would have written it. It would have stood in that book tonight, under a card with one name on it, and gone up to a counter by the fourth bell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:181:Cael saw it from the gallery, over Brom's shoulder, before the man at the desk had turned his page. There were two chairs at that desk, of which one stood before the slope, and had a cushion on it, flattened by more years than he could guess into the shape of one person. The other stood against the wall at the desk's side. It had been heaped yesterday, like every flat thing in that place, with bundles to the height of its back; and it was bare now, and somebody had wiped it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:Karis saw it, and did not sit; she stood by it with the flat under her arm until the grey head came round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:191:So it went to Lira, and she held it for an hour in her right hand, with her elbow on her knee; and at the end of the hour the flame had begun to shiver, by so little that Cael could tell it only from the edge of a shadow on the Ring's third sheet. She looked at her own wrist as though it had spoken out of turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:199:He went down on his left knee, a hand to the left of the place where two red eyes were resting, and laid his right forearm along his thigh, and let the lamp hang from two fingers as a mason lets a plumb-bob hang. Then he did the one thing that he knew how to do in such a case: he let his breath go long. He counted it, as a healer had taught him on a plank, to ten and to ten again, and he gave over wanting to be anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:201:He knew how to be a post, which was most of what he had learned since a plate lit. He had stood still in the corners of halls so that he should not be looked at, and at the edges of yards. He had stood locked on a bank of scree for a whole count, with six hounds coming and not a finger that would answer him. He had never before been asked for it as a skill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:205:Nothing was said for a long while. The man went along the Ring's eleven again, and he did it otherwise than yesterday: he did not go sheet by sheet now, but across them, from a mark on one to a mark on its neighbour, with his head moving a little from side to side. When he moved, Cael moved, a hand to the left. He found that he could tell where the eyes would go next by the set of the shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:217:That was all that he gave her for it. Karis wrote nothing; she sat forward on a bare chair with her gloved hands shut, and watched him as she would have watched a pot that she was not allowed to stir.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:235:And he was two feet from it. He had wished, in a wood-yard, to see how that man looked at a thing; and he saw it now for hours together, from closer than Karis sat, with every sheet lying foot toward him and wrong way up, as he had read every paper of his life. He could not see what the eyes found. He could only see that they found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:"I'm going up for an hour. Set it on the table's end. Touch nothing." He went by them toward the stair with his eyes already half shut. "There's a jar of water on the shelf. You may have what's in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:247:"He cleared the other," said Karis. She had not got up from it. She laid one glove flat on the wiped wood, beside her knee. "He did that last night, after we had gone up. I do not suppose that anybody has sat here in his lifetime."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:255:He had no frame. He had given it back to the man that he hired it of, and paid the copper; and he looked exactly as he had looked on every other evening, which Cael thought must have taken some doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:281:Nobody added to it. Brom went down for the pot, and brought it up on a board, with seven bowls, and set the seventh at the end by itself; and the old man took his standing and thanked him once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:285:He took out Hesk's book when the bowls had gone, and set the day at its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:289:He looked at that last line, and let it stand, since it was the first use that anybody had found for him inside a wall. Then he wrote the rest, and it came all in one piece, as it did when he had carried a thing the whole day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:291:*Karis has been looking for this room her whole life. Cleon's been sitting in it for thirty years waiting for her. Vastin tried to open a door with his rank and nearly closed all of them — an Archmarshal's worth nothing here, which he's taking about as well as I took [SHATTERED]. Down here the stone's older than the Compact, so the Compact doesn't reach. First place inside walls I've ever stood where nobody can file me, and it's a hole under a city, and it's because everything down here is older than the machine that files. Karis says give her a week. I've got a box to carry and a promise to keep about a station upstairs. I can wait.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:293:He turned the book round. Lira read it with her chin down and her lips going, and stopped at the third line, and looked across the lantern at a brown coat by the gable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:295:"That's not well," she said, very low. "You've put that he's taking it as you did. You took it like a cat takes a bath."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:303:"No," said Lira. "That was the trouble with it." She lay down on her pallet, on the right side, with the slow arm uppermost. "I held that lamp an hour. My hand's still going. How do you do it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:Cael marked it only because it was the heaviest thing there. It had two wheels as tall as Lira, and one mule between the shafts, and it was loaded to the rails with roofing slate, stacked on edge in straw. A lad of twelve or so walked at the mule's head. A man in a good grey cloak and a broad hat walked beside the tail-board, with a tally in his hand; and there was a square of slate painted on the dray's side, which was some broker's sign.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:7:Loads came into that yard all day, and Cael had learned in two mornings how it took them. Whatever had wheels came up from the bottom, past the beam, where a man with a slate wrote a weight against an owner; whatever had legs came down the slot in the wall, as they had, and was written nowhere until it reached a hatch. The yard sloped between the two a good deal more steeply than a carter would have chosen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:9:The four of them had come out of the cut a moment before, and Karis had gone to the hatch with the card. Brom had set his box down by the bench. He was looking at the dray as he looked at any load, to see how it had been put together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:13:It was the near wheel that went. Cael saw the pin jump from its hole and ring on the stones; and after that the wheel walked off its arm, quite slowly, as a drunken man leaves a doorway, and rolled away down the yard by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:15:The dray dropped on that corner; its bed tipped, and the whole stack of slate slid to the low side in one long clattering rush, like a dresser of plates going over. The mule screamed and plunged. The lad had let go of its head and jumped back toward the dock, as anybody would have; and the bed's rail came down across his boot and held it against the kerb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:25:He had not raised his voice, and it carried all the same, as a bell carries. He was walking, not running, with his eyes on the heap of slate; and Cael, who had watched him read a wall a hundred times, saw him read this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:31:*What does it weigh?* asked the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:It was the third of the ten that he carried, a piece of Brom's own Iron come to him at second hand, and it asked under the soles of Cael's feet, low and even, as it had asked of every rut for nine days; it asked also which way the weight was going, since that was the half of its trade. It could have told him to a stone. It could have told him which slate would move next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:39:"Lira. His belt, at the back. Both hands, if you've got them. Not till I say, and when I say, you take him straight toward yourself and you don't stop." He did not look round. "Cael. The wood wagon. Two splits, thick, as long as your arm; and one of those men to drive them. Under the axle first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:It was the good one. The right was in four yards of webbing, under his shirt, with another woman's knot upon it, and he held that arm across his thigh as a man keeps a tool out of the way. He set his left foot flat, and his hand on the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:55:He had seen it come up a dozen times: on sprung oak, and in a hall, and on a road with hounds. It began under the collar and went over the skin like frost over a pane, and when it had gone over, a blade would not mark the man. A dray would have been nothing to him then. He would have stood up under it as under a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:67:Lira had the belt in her right hand and the left hooked beside it, a beat behind, as it was behind in everything. She threw her whole weight backward. The lad came out from the kerb like a cork, and sat down on the paving with her; and his boot stayed where it was, under the rail, empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:It had. The lifting had tipped the heap again, and a dozen plates had run down into the corner all together; and what Brom had raised by two fingers was now coming down upon him with a good deal more than he had taken up. Cael saw his knee begin to open. He could not set the thing down. The kerb was still under the rail, where a foot had been, and Lira's hands were on the stones beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:77:The yard-man drove the first split under the axle's stub with the heel of his hand, and Cael the second beneath the bed's rail, end on, and kicked it home. The dray settled on them. It creaked, and took the oak a little into its timber, and was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:79:Brom came out from under it on his hands, and stood up slowly. He tried to lift the left arm, and it went as high as the elbow would carry it, and stopped; and he looked at it with the deep patient dislike that he had once given to a hound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:Cael turned, and the mule was standing. It had been throwing itself about in the shafts a moment since, and every plunge had rocked the bed. It stood now with its head down and its sides going, and an old man had hold of its cheek-strap. He had the stick in his right hand. The left, which would not open flat, had shut on the leather; shutting was the one thing that it could still do well. He was looking at nobody; he had held a ferry's stern so once, in a flood, with a turn round a post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:It was a real offer, and Cael knew what it weighed. There was a house in Velmere that had wanted exactly this for its son, for as long as it had had him: a coat with somebody's colour on it, and a place inside a wall, and a figure at the month's end that could be written in a letter. A panel had sat once to tell that house what he was worth. Here a stranger had told him in a yard, after watching his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:She had said that she would, at the door, with the lamp in her hand; and she had looked at Brom's right shoulder as she said it, and then at Cael. So he came down last but one, with nothing to do but watch a box ride where a healer had said that nothing was to ride. It sat well. Brom carried it as he would have carried it on the other side, and if the knot spoke to him, he did not pass on what it said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:159:That was all of it. Cael had been afraid, on a climbing street, of a blue coat that would read a girl's name aloud. He had not thought to be afraid of a grey cloak; and it had come and gone in a yard, while he knelt at an axle, and he had not been asked to do anything about it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:He saw it from the gallery's mouth, with the carried lamp still thirty strides ahead of him: a long dark thing stood on its end against the table's edge, beside the Hall's fourth sheet, with a fold of sacking on the floor at its foot. It was a plank. It was of oak, as black as the shelves, as long as a man's arm and two hands broad; and as the light came up he saw that one face of it had been cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:167:There were four rows. They ran across the board's width, small and square and deep, and he knew them as a person knows the letters of his own tongue in a strange hand: he had seen their kind on three floors, by lantern, in her pencil. At the end of each row the cutter had made something else, a cluster of little pecks and rings; and after the cluster, a few signs more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:171:"No," said the voice from the desk. A leaf was turned face down. "It was on the bottom shelf of the west gallery, under a heap of quarrymen's wedges, with its face to the wall. I've had it ten years." The chair scraped. "Lamp."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:179:The bowls held counters: white pebbles in one, and black in another, and in the third a heap of small red squares that had been broken from a tile. The reed was as long as his forearm. He did not touch paper with his skin, and Cael had seen by now that he did not mean to; the reed did it for him, from a hand's breadth off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:181:"Top row of that sheet," he said to the chair. "I'll point. You lay. White where I say white."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:183:He pointed, and Karis laid. A white pebble went down on the first sign of the row. Three black followed it, on the next three. Then red, and red; and white again; and black. She set each one below its mark, not upon it, so that her own pencil should not be hidden. It went the length of the row, twelve signs, and when it was done there were twelve counters in a line across the foot of her drawing: two white, seven black, three red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:187:The reed went to the plank's first row. He said the colours again, and she laid them along the table's edge beneath it; and Cael, who had the best seat in the gallery for it, saw the second line of pebbles grow beside the first. White. Three black. Red, and red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:195:"It bears." The reed touched the air over a white pebble. "Take that out and the row comes down. There's nothing left for the rest to hang on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:199:"Hangs. It's carried. You could change one of those for another kind, and the row'd still stand up; it'd only be carrying something different." The reed moved. "The red's nothing. It holds nothing and nothing holds it. It's there so that the row fills its stone from edge to edge, as a mason packs a joint."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:201:"How d'you know?" said Brom. He had come to stand at the table's far corner, with his right arm folded and his left hanging. "They're scratches. One's as deep as the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside a wall on a moor that had roared like a forge with its door open. This made no sound at all. The man only looked, from the foot of a thing upward, without blinking, while the water ran down beside his nose; and whatever it was that he did, it went into the oak as quietly as ink goes into paper. It was the stillest thing that Cael had ever known a person do with what a plate had given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:He could not have said what he had looked for in it. A Path, as he had met them, was a thing that was done to somebody: each of the ten in him had come out of a blow, or a throw, or a pair of hands laid on to mend, and he had learned every one of them by what it cost the person at the other end. This was done to nobody. It was only a man attending, as hard as a man can, to something that another man had made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:"To do a thing; to what; and how many times." The stick's foot turned on the rock. "I have drawn some thousands of papers in that form, and had as many drawn for me. I have followed fifty-two of them down a stair for three days, on other people's backs; and it did not once enter my mind that I was looking at my own trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:259:He said it as a carter states his rate, and then he went up for his bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:265:She did it without a pebble, on a leaf of her notebook, going from sheet to sheet of the Hall's twelve with the pencil held upright. She had forty-one kinds of sign, and had had them for the better part of a year; and she knew now, of the twelve that stood in one row, which kind bore and which was carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:267:"I shall want every row," she said. "He will have to go through all fifty-two with that reed, and I shall have to lay each one and write it, before I can say how many kinds are white."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:273:The man came down within the hour, and it was done as she had said. He went along the Hall's table through the afternoon with the reed, and she laid, and wrote; and between the sheets he went back to the plank. The second of its rows stood on the Stair's ninth sheet, nine signs long, and the third on the Ring's sixth. He found each as a carpenter finds a joint in the dark, by going to where it ought to be. Each time the reed came back to the plank, and the pebbles went down along the table's edge in the order in which they had gone down on paper, white and black and red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:275:The fourth row was the shortest, of seven signs, and it stood near the foot of the Stair's twenty-first sheet. When he had found it he straightened, and put both hands in the small of his back. Cleon looked from the plank to the three tables, and at the plank again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:279:Cael kept the flame where it was wanted, and did not hear what Karis answered. He saw her glove come down upon the table's edge, flat, an inch from her own pencil, where she had been told that nobody was to lean; and the man beside her looked at the glove, and for once did not say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:301:The dray was gone from the yard when they crossed it. Its two logs lay by the dock, where somebody had thrown them, and the wheel had been found. Brom looked at the place in passing, as a man looks at a piece of work that he has finished and been paid for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:305:*Brom: the left shoulder wrenched, under a dray in the registry's yard, with no Path opened. He is carrying on the right, in the old healer's knot, against Oryn's word at the fork. I did not tell him to put it down. I couldn't have made him. Owed at the mill, to two healers, by me as much as by him. Entered.*

