editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:205:The man had come out of the long house with his copper spent. He had stood under the lantern at the byre's door, looking down at the linen on his knee, and his right hand had gone to the breast of the brown coat, where a pen would have been. Cael knew the gesture by now. An officer hurt on duty wrote it up within the day, no doubt, under two hands, with the circumstances; there would be a sheet for it, and a tray. The hand had found a receipt in a small woman's writing, and nothing else. It had stayed there a moment. Then it had come away empty, and Vastin had looked up at the black shoulder of the hill, and for the space of three breaths his face had had nothing in it that Cael had ever seen there: no room that he was being fair to, and no file.
