editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:3621:He had had a name once that was only a name. A plate had put a word after it, in a building with no windows; and from that day he had not been able to give the one without the other arriving behind it, like a dog behind a cart. So he had learned to say the word himself. He had said it first, and flatly, across every counter that he came to, and looked the clerk in the eye while he did it, so that it should cost the man something to write. It had been the only blade that he owned. He had carried it for longer than he liked to count; and he had laid it down for the first time yesterday, at that same hatch, and been a pair of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:3846:Cael had priced that man a great many ways since a yard above Ostrand. He had priced him by what he could carry, and by what he would take on his own skin for somebody else, and by a figure that a board had given him and a house had declined to sign; and he saw now that every one of those sums had had the grey in it. He had never once asked what the man was worth with the Path shut. It appeared to be a dray of slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:3892:It was a real offer, and Cael knew what it weighed. There was a house in Velmere that had wanted exactly this for its son, for as long as it had had him: a coat with somebody's colour on it, and a place inside a wall, and a figure at the month's end that could be written in a letter. A panel had sat once to tell that house what he was worth. Here a stranger had told him in a yard, after watching his neck.
