+## Protected/restored units and lane joins
+
+These are the joins in this range identified by `protected-patterns.txt`, the lane reports, or the coordinator's rulings. Quotations below preserve the manuscript exactly. Each join was heard in its full scene.
+
+### Protected and restored units
+
+1. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`, protected clear/empty/safe line
+   - Before: `He was wet to the shins still, and he was looking down at a piece of black water the shape of a slate, as he looked at a paper that he had finished with.`
+   - Unit: `"It's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe," said Seln.`
+   - After: `The factor turned to look at him, and found nothing to dispute, and came down off the bank.`
+   - Ruling: sound. Seln is named in the unit and the factor's reaction follows without ambiguity.
+
+2. `manuscript/chapter-19.md`, protected debt line
+   - Before: `She looked at the hand that was doing it, and drew the sleeve down.`
+   - Unit: `"That's on me," said Oryn. "It's the one thing I'm walking up that road owing, and I'll pay it."`
+   - After: `"Then it'll keep," said Cael.`
+   - Ruling: sound. Both turns are tagged.
+
+3. `manuscript/chapter-19.md`, protected steps/square line
+   - Before: `Then she stood a moment before Cael, with her free hand shut, at her own distance.`
+   - Unit: `"Not the steps," she said. "Not the square."`
+   - After: `She turned the mule's head to the north.`
+   - Ruling: sound. The prior sentence fixes `she` as Oryn and the departure completes the beat.
+
+4. `manuscript/chapter-20.md`, protected cord/stone line
+   - Before: `It had nothing to go along with, and so it said nothing at all; and for the better part of a year he had been writing that nothing down in pencil, with the day at its head, as though it had been an answer.`
+   - Unit: `He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*.`
+   - After: `A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go.`
+   - Ruling: sound. The metaphor develops in one direction and italics add no required vocal distinction.
+
+5. `manuscript/chapter-23.md`, protected chapter-end exchange
+   - Before: `The blanket hung over the gable, with a faint paleness round its edges that did not flicker; and Cael lay back, with the book shut on his chest and his eyes on the slates a yard above him.`
+   - Unit: `"Last time we walked into a place like this," Cael said, "it took four years and a hearing to walk out."` through `"A name and a reason," Karis said. "Older than the walls, under our feet, and nobody's read it because nobody's had a reason to look and a person who could read it in the same lifetime."`
+   - After: none; the protected exchange closes the chapter.
+   - Ruling: sound. The Cael/Karis alternation makes the tagless `Do we now?` unambiguous.
+
+6. `manuscript/chapter-24.md`, protected inventory
+   - Before: `"Off floors," Cleon said.`
+   - Unit: `"Fused. Grey. No join. Three of them. I copied every mark on all three. Fifty-two sheets," Karis said.`
+   - After: `"Fifty-two sheets," Cleon said.`
+   - Ruling: sound. The repetition is deliberate confirmation, not a doubled join.
+
+7. `manuscript/chapter-26.md`, protected four-hand exchange
+   - Before: `"It's worth the look," she said. "I've looked."`
+   - Unit: `"Four hands," she said.\n\n"Four."\n\n"Right."`
+   - After: `It was Cael who had given her the figure.`
